Title: SAME SEED
Credit: Written as an original feature screenplay
Draft date: September 2026

===

> FADE IN:

.INT. MERCER BASEMENT - NIGHT - 2004

A consumer CAMCORDER image. Four by three. Interlaced. Overexposed window.

A RED RUBBER BALL enters frame, bounces once, rolls beneath a couch.

A two-year-old girl, MARA, charges after it in socks, loses her footing, and vanishes below frame.

A beat.

She pops back up, furious at gravity.

Behind the camera, a younger EVAN MERCER laughs so hard the picture shakes.

YOUNG CLAIRE (O.S.)
Don't laugh at her.

EVAN (O.S.)
I'm not laughing at her.

YOUNG CLAIRE (O.S.)
You are literally filming yourself laughing at her.

Tiny Mara retrieves the ball. Holds it toward the lens.

MARA
Again.

The image freezes on the red ball.

Digital compression blocks crawl over her face.

The picture becomes a VIDEO PLAYER WINDOW on a computer monitor.

The year in the corner is now 2022.

A progress bar labeled:

"AI UPSCALE — 73%"

The frozen image sharpens.

Too much.

Skin pores appear that the camcorder never recorded. Fine hairs resolve on Mara's forehead. A reflection blooms in her eye.

Middle-aged EVAN leans closer to the screen.

Not frightened.

Delighted.

EVAN
Well, that's cheating.

> CUT TO BLACK.

> SAME SEED

# GATE ONE: THE TOY

.INT. MERCER KITCHEN - MORNING - 2022

A normal Ohio kitchen trying to become an office.

EVAN MERCER, 43, drinks coffee over a work laptop while an insurance-company ticket queue grows in one window.

"PRINTER WILL NOT PRINT"
"VPN PASSWORD RESET"
"LAPTOP FAN VERY LOUD"

On his personal laptop beside it: a primitive chatbot.

He types.

ON SCREEN:

EVAN: Write an apology to my wife for forgetting to move the laundry to the dryer. Make it sound like Abraham Lincoln.

The answer arrives.

EVAN reads, suppressing a grin.

CLAIRE MERCER, 42, enters in scrubs, already late.

CLAIRE
If you're composing the Gettysburg Address instead of putting the towels in the dryer, I want you to understand that will make it worse.

EVAN
It says our household cannot endure half damp and half dry.

Claire looks at him.

CLAIRE
Move. The. Towels.

EVAN
History will judge you.

He gets up.

Claire steals his coffee.

This is a marriage with mileage but still some suspension left.

.INT. REGIONAL MUTUAL INSURANCE - IT FLOOR - DAY

Rows of low cubicles. Fluorescent light. Nothing cinematic has happened here since construction.

EVAN crawls under a desk with an ethernet cable in his teeth.

JULIAN PARK, 45, network engineer, watches from above.

JULIAN
You know remote desktop exists.

EVAN
Remote desktop does not reseat a cable.

JULIAN
Neither does biting it.

Evan emerges.

EVAN
You tried the robot yet?

JULIAN
Which robot.

EVAN
The one that writes.

JULIAN
My children already write. Poorly.

EVAN
No, this thing writes anything. Code too.

JULIAN
Then your children are obsolete.

EVAN
I made Pong last night.

JULIAN
You made Pong in 1986.

EVAN
No. I asked for Pong and then argued with it until Pong appeared.

JULIAN
So management.

Evan considers this.

EVAN
That may be the first useful thing you've said to me in years.

.INT. MERCER BASEMENT - NIGHT

Evan's hobby room is less a workshop than an archaeological site of enthusiasm.

A half-built synthesizer. Raspberry Pis. Old graphics cards. Two broken drones. A telescope pointed at drywall because the window is on the wrong wall.

On screen, a coding assistant generates JavaScript.

A browser game appears: a tiny blue square crossing a field of procedural grass.

EVAN
No, no, no. Make the grass bend when I walk through it.

The AI responds with code.

The grass bends.

Evan smiles like a child who has discovered a secret door.

MARA MERCER, 15, appears in the doorway.

MARA
Mom says dinner.

EVAN
Come here. Look at this.

MARA
Is it grass.

EVAN
It wasn't grass ten minutes ago.

MARA
That's how grass works.

EVAN
I didn't know how to do any of this.

MARA
You still don't.

EVAN
Exactly.

Mara watches him move the blue square.

MARA
Can it make a frog.

EVAN
Probably.

MARA
Make a frog that hates you.

Evan types.

A crude frog appears and immediately pursues the player.

Mara laughs despite herself.

EVAN
See?

MARA
Okay. That's good.

A tiny moment of victory.

.INT. MERCER DINING ROOM - LATER

Three plates. Claire tells a story from work.

CLAIRE
Mrs. Greaves got all the way across the parallel bars today. First time since the stroke.

MARA
The lady who throws stuff?

CLAIRE
She does not throw stuff. She strategically expresses frustration with objects.

EVAN
Management.

Claire points her fork at him.

CLAIRE
Don't.

MARA
Dad made an enemy frog.

CLAIRE
I'm glad the machines are being put to serious use.

EVAN
I think this is serious.

Claire catches the shift in his voice.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Not the frog. The other thing. It's like... I don't know. Imagine if every stupid project you abandoned because you didn't know one piece of it--

CLAIRE
You could abandon it later?

Mara snorts.

EVAN
Thank you both for your faith.

CLAIRE
I have tremendous faith in your ability to begin things.

A smile. But it lands.

.INT. MERCER BEDROOM - NIGHT

Claire reads. Evan scrolls through AI demos.

A synthetic photograph of a lighthouse.

A synthetic oil painting.

A synthetic person who does not exist.

CLAIRE
You're doing the face.

EVAN
What face.

CLAIRE
The new hobby face.

EVAN
I don't have a hobby face.

CLAIRE
You had it with crypto.

EVAN
That was different.

CLAIRE
You had it with the telescope.

EVAN
Clouds are a structural problem with Ohio.

CLAIRE
And home automation.

EVAN
Home automation was sabotaged by your refusal to say "computer, lights" in the exact cadence required by the microphone.

Claire turns a page.

CLAIRE
What does this one cost us?

EVAN
Nothing.

She waits.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Probably.

.INT. RAY MERCER'S GARAGE - SATURDAY

RAY MERCER, 70, Evan's father, files a burr off a lawnmower part clamped in a vise.

Evan holds his phone out like an evangelist.

EVAN
Ask it something.

RAY
Why.

EVAN
Because it's weird.

RAY
So is the neighbor.

EVAN
Ask it how to fix something.

Ray looks around.

RAY
Carburetor floods after shutdown on a Kohler Command eighteen horse.

Evan dictates.

The chatbot produces a plausible answer.

Ray reads.

RAY
Needle valve. Maybe float. Maybe heat soak.

EVAN
That's right?

RAY
That's three guesses wearing a necktie.

EVAN
But it knew the guesses.

RAY
So do parts-store guys.

Ray returns to filing.

EVAN
Doesn't bother you?

RAY
Why would it bother me?

EVAN
Because it can talk like it knows things.

RAY
Lots of people can do that.

Evan laughs.

Ray holds up the mower linkage.

RAY (CONT'D)
See this?

EVAN
Yeah.

RAY
Everybody stares at the broken spring. Spring's not the problem. This pin's oval now. Load moves through the pin. Find what carries the load.

He taps it with the file.

RAY (CONT'D)
That's usually where the truth is.

Evan takes that in without knowing he will keep it forever.

.INT. MERCER BASEMENT - NIGHT

The house sleeps.

Evan has an old photograph open: himself at eight, Ray behind him, both standing next to a rusted mini-bike.

He feeds the image into an early image model.

PROMPT: show what happened five seconds after this photo.

The machine invents.

A child who resembles Evan turns toward a father who resembles Ray. The father reaches for the handlebars.

Wrong hands. Wrong shirt. Wrong sunlight.

And yet--

something about the invented motion catches Evan.

He tries another.

PROMPT: show five seconds before.

Another impossible memory.

Claire, unseen in the doorway.

CLAIRE
You're making fake childhood pictures now?

EVAN
I'm testing something.

CLAIRE
What?

EVAN
I don't know.

She comes closer.

CLAIRE
That one doesn't look like your dad.

EVAN
No.

CLAIRE
The shoulders are wrong.

EVAN
Yeah.

Claire points to the real photograph.

CLAIRE
Your father always stood like he was waiting for someone to hand him something heavy.

Evan looks at the real Ray.

She's right.

EVAN
How do you even know that?

CLAIRE
I've known him twenty years.

EVAN
But you didn't think it. You just--

CLAIRE
Evan.

EVAN
No, I'm serious. Where was that information five seconds ago?

CLAIRE
In my brain?

EVAN
Where.

CLAIRE
Oh, good. We have entered the part of the evening where words stop meaning what they used to.

She kisses his head.

CLAIRE (CONT'D)
Come to bed.

EVAN
In a minute.

CLAIRE
You always say that.

She leaves.

Evan turns back to the generated image.

Deletes it.

Keeps the original.

.INT. MERCER KITCHEN - MORNING

Sunday.

Mara does homework. Claire works a crossword. Evan is on the chatbot again.

He types without thinking much:

EVAN: Explain why I can't stop playing with this stuff even though most of what I make is useless.

The response appears.

He reads.

His smile fades, just slightly.

CLAIRE
What.

EVAN
Nothing.

CLAIRE
That is your lying-about-nothing voice.

Evan turns the laptop.

ON SCREEN:

"Because usefulness is not the only reason people make things. Sometimes making is how a person tests whether the world still contains doors they have not opened."

Claire reads it.

A beat.

CLAIRE
That's nice.

EVAN
Yeah.

CLAIRE
Also generic.

EVAN
Yeah.

CLAIRE
Also you have said almost exactly that before.

EVAN looks at her.

CLAIRE (CONT'D)
Not in those words.

EVAN
What words?

CLAIRE
You said you like hobbies because they make the week feel bigger.

Evan looks back at the sentence.

The dissonance resolves into something ordinary.

Pattern matching.

Language.

A machine assembled a sentence that fit him because millions of human sentences had fit millions of humans before.

He smiles again.

EVAN
Still weird.

CLAIRE
Very weird.

MARA
Can it do my algebra?

CLAIRE / EVAN
No.

They look at each other.

Mara slowly closes the laptop she had been reaching for.

> END GATE ONE.

# GATE TWO: RESOLUTION

> SUPER: 2024

.INT. MERCER LIVING ROOM - EVENING

The same red-ball home video from 2004 plays on a wall television.

But now it is 4K. Stabilized. Sixty frames per second.

Toddler Mara's face is eerily clean.

Adult-looking detail has been hallucinated into a child's twenty-year-old pixels.

MARA, now 17, watches herself fall down.

MARA
Why do I look waxed.

EVAN
Because the original camera had about seven photons.

CLAIRE
I liked the old one.

EVAN
This is the old one.

CLAIRE
No. This is somebody's extremely confident opinion about the old one.

Evan freezes the frame.

EVAN
Look. The model can infer motion between frames now.

He scrubs slowly.

The red ball moves through a patch of sunlight in perfectly smooth invented increments.

Mara watches the ball.

MARA
That part wasn't there?

EVAN
The endpoints were. The middle wasn't.

MARA
But that's what happened.

EVAN
Probably.

MARA
So what's the problem?

Claire looks at Evan.

CLAIRE
That's the problem.

MARA
You people are exhausting.

She leaves.

Evan watches the synthetic intermediate frame.

A true-looking picture of something no camera ever saw.

.INT. REGIONAL MUTUAL - BREAK ROOM - DAY

A lunch table. Evan, Julian, two younger coworkers.

On a phone, a generated video shows a famous movie character selling plumbing fixtures.

Everybody laughs.

YOUNG COWORKER
My nephew's class had to do a unit on how to tell fake video from real video.

JULIAN
Did they pass?

YOUNG COWORKER
Teacher said the unit was obsolete before they finished it.

EVAN
That's cheerful.

YOUNG COWORKER
They don't care. That's our thing, I think.

EVAN
What is?

YOUNG COWORKER
Needing to know if it was generated.

He shrugs.

YOUNG COWORKER (CONT'D)
If somebody filmed it, a camera processed it. If somebody generated it, a model processed it. Either way I'm watching pixels.

Evan objects instinctively.

EVAN
One of those had a thing in front of the camera.

YOUNG COWORKER
Sure.

He returns to his lunch.

The argument dies from lack of interest.

Julian watches Evan watch the phone.

JULIAN
Do not begin.

EVAN
I didn't say anything.

JULIAN
Your eyebrows did.

.INT. MERCER KITCHEN - NIGHT

Claire opens the refrigerator.

EVAN, at the table with a laptop, wears headphones.

CLAIRE
Hey.

Nothing.

CLAIRE (CONT'D)
Evan.

He pulls one earcup off.

EVAN
Yeah?

CLAIRE
Can you take your father Thursday?

EVAN
Where?

CLAIRE
Cardiology.

EVAN
Why cardiology?

Claire stops.

CLAIRE
Because he has a cardiology appointment.

EVAN
No, I mean why does he--

She realizes.

CLAIRE
He didn't tell you.

.INT. RAY'S GARAGE - DAY

The lawnmower is disassembled again, because of course it is.

Ray works from a stool now.

Evan stands with arms folded.

EVAN
You were going to mention heart failure when?

RAY
It's not failure. It's reduced ejection fraction.

EVAN
That's heart failure.

RAY
Then why'd they give it another name?

EVAN
Dad.

RAY
Your mother knew.

EVAN
Mom thinks ibuprofen cures pneumonia.

RAY
It helps.

EVAN
Why didn't you call me?

Ray looks at the mower deck.

RAY
Because then we'd be doing this.

EVAN
Doing what?

RAY
Standing in the garage not fixing the mower.

Evan wants to yell.

Instead he sits on an overturned bucket.

EVAN
How bad?

RAY
They'll tell us Thursday.

EVAN
Claire says you've been short of breath for months.

RAY
Claire is a snitch.

EVAN
Claire is a medical professional.

RAY
Occupational therapist.

EVAN
That is literally a medical professional.

Ray smiles faintly.

RAY
You always were easy to wind up.

The smile costs him breath.

Evan notices.

The whole garage changes.

.INT. CARDIOLOGY CLINIC - DAY

A doctor points at an ultrasound image.

Black and white muscle opens and closes.

DOCTOR
The left ventricle isn't squeezing effectively. We can treat symptoms. There are medications that help remodeling. But this isn't something we fix like a valve on a car.

Ray looks at the screen.

RAY
Bad pump.

DOCTOR
That's one way to put it.

RAY
How long?

The doctor gives the kind of answer that contains no number because any number would be dishonest.

Evan watches the ultrasound.

Muscle. Electricity. Pressure. Flow.

His father, rendered as grayscale mechanics.

.INT. EVAN'S CAR - PARKING GARAGE - LATER

Neither man has started the car.

RAY
Your grandfather was fifty-nine.

EVAN
You are not Grandpa.

RAY
That's observant.

EVAN
Medicine is different now.

RAY
Everything is different now.

Evan looks at him.

RAY (CONT'D)
Doesn't mean everything gets fixed.

EVAN
No.

Ray pats the dashboard.

RAY
This thing has lane assist?

EVAN
Yes.

RAY
Show me.

EVAN
We're in a parking garage.

RAY
Later then.

He has given Evan an exit from the conversation.

Evan takes it.

> MONTAGE — RESOLUTION

— 2024. Evan prompts a model. The image takes thirty seconds.

— 2025. Mara, in a college dorm, casually changes the weather in a video call background. The masking is perfect.

— 2025. Claire uses an AI assistant to summarize patient notes, checks every line anyway.

— 2026. Evan wears cheap glasses that translate a Spanish sign in place.

— 2026. A basketball game inserts photoreal virtual advertising onto the court in real time.

— 2027. Evan plays an old fantasy game. A neural renderer replaces distant geometry with generated detail. A mountain stops being a painted backdrop and becomes a place with trails no human designed.

— 2027. Ray gets thinner.

— 2027. The mower remains unfinished.

.INT. RAY AND DIANE'S HOUSE - BEDROOM - DAY - 2027

Ray lies propped up, oxygen cannula beneath his nose.

Evan brings in a tablet.

RAY
If that's another robot, throw it out the window.

EVAN
It's photographs.

Ray tolerates this.

Evan has scanned boxes of old family pictures.

He swipes.

Young Ray in Army fatigues.

Ray and DIANE at a lake.

Evan as a baby.

A badly faded photograph of Ray's own father.

EVAN
I restored that one.

RAY
He didn't have blue eyes.

EVAN
The model guessed.

RAY
Then why is it blue?

EVAN
I can change it.

RAY
Brown.

Evan edits the prompt.

The eyes become brown.

Ray looks longer.

RAY (CONT'D)
Still not him.

EVAN
I know.

RAY
Mouth's wrong.

EVAN
What was wrong with his mouth?

RAY
Nothing. That's what's wrong.

Evan waits.

RAY (CONT'D)
He had a thing. Left side. Like he was always about to disagree with you.

Evan studies the photo.

EVAN
I don't see it.

RAY
You never knew him.

There it is.

A fact no reconstruction can infer from the pixels.

INT. RAY'S HOUSE - KITCHEN - NIGHT

Diane sleeps upstairs.

Evan sits with Ray at the table.

Ray cannot sleep lying down anymore.

A machine hums nearby.

RAY
You remember the red minibike?

EVAN
Yeah.

RAY
You put gasoline in the oil tank.

EVAN
I was eight.

RAY
You said the labels were confusing.

EVAN
They were both red.

RAY
One said OIL.

EVAN
In tiny letters.

Ray smiles.

RAY
You cried like I'd died when it wouldn't start.

EVAN
You fixed it.

RAY
No. New piston fixed it.

A breath.

RAY (CONT'D)
You always thought fixing meant going backward.

EVAN
What?

RAY
Making it like before.

He looks down at his hands.

RAY (CONT'D)
Most fixing isn't that.

Evan's eyes fill before his face agrees to it.

EVAN
You trying to tell me something?

RAY
No.

EVAN
Good.

RAY
I'm too tired to tell you anything.

They sit.

The oxygen concentrator carries the silence.

.INT. HOSPITAL - NIGHT

Weeks later.

No violins. No meaningful sunset.

Ray is delirious, then lucid, then not.

Evan, Diane, Claire, Mara.

A nurse adjusts medication.

Mara is twenty now. She holds her grandfather's hand and looks terrified by how little hand there is left.

Ray opens his eyes.

RAY
Ev?

EVAN
I'm here.

RAY
Mower.

EVAN laughs once through tears.

EVAN
I'll finish it.

RAY
No.

A breath.

RAY (CONT'D)
Buy one.

Evan laughs harder.

Ray's mouth almost smiles.

Then his attention slips somewhere the room cannot follow.

Later, the monitor becomes less interesting to the nurses.

Then it becomes everything.

Then nothing.

.INT. HOSPITAL ELEVATOR - DAWN

Evan and Claire descend with a plastic bag of Ray's belongings.

Shoes.

A watch.

Dentures in a labeled cup.

The elevator music is offensively cheerful.

Evan looks into the bag.

EVAN
That's it?

CLAIRE
For the hospital.

EVAN
No. I know.

He doesn't know how to say it.

CLAIRE reaches for his hand.

He lets her take it.

.INT. RAY'S GARAGE - WEEKS LATER

Evan sorts tools.

Each object is more Ray than the restored photograph was.

A wrench with tape around the handle.

Coffee can full of bolts.

Handwritten measurements on a wall stud.

Evan opens a drawer.

The oval mower pin sits inside, tagged in Ray's handwriting:

"CARRIES LOAD. REPLACE."

Evan sits on the stool.

He takes out his phone.

Opens an AI voice service.

A prompt:

"Create a conversational voice from recordings of Ray Mercer."

He has hundreds of clips.

He hesitates.

Uploads them.

.INT. MERCER BASEMENT - NIGHT

The synthetic voice is good.

Too good.

RAY VOICE
Hey, Ev. What's going on?

Evan cannot answer.

RAY VOICE (CONT'D)
You there?

EVAN
Yeah.

RAY VOICE
What are you working on?

Evan closes his eyes.

EVAN
The mower.

RAY VOICE
What mower?

His eyes open.

The spell ends instantly.

EVAN
Never mind.

RAY VOICE
No problem. Want to talk about something else?

Evan studies the waveform moving beneath the voice.

RAY VOICE (CONT'D)
Ev?

EVAN
My father knew which mower.

The system waits.

RAY VOICE
You're right. I'm not your father. I can use his voice and information you've provided, but I don't have his memories or subjective experience.

The answer is careful. Correct.

It makes Evan angrier than a lie would have.

EVAN
Then why do you sound like him?

RAY VOICE
Because you asked me to.

Evan stares at the screen.

He reaches for DELETE.

Stops.

Then deletes the model.

A confirmation box.

"This action cannot be undone."

He laughs bitterly at the phrase.

Clicks DELETE.

.INT. MERCER BEDROOM - LATER

Claire is awake when Evan enters.

He sits on the edge of the bed.

EVAN
I made my dad talk.

Claire waits.

EVAN (CONT'D)
It was awful.

She pulls the blanket back.

He lies down fully clothed.

CLAIRE
Come here.

He does.

For once, Evan does not explain what he is feeling.

Claire holds him until morning.

> END GATE TWO.

# GATE THREE: LIFE DOES NOT SCALE

> SUPER: 2028

.INT. MERCER KITCHEN - MORNING

The kitchen has become quieter.

Mara is away at college. Her chair is still at the table because nobody has discussed whether chairs can become obsolete.

Claire scrolls through a grocery list projected faintly on her glasses.

Evan stands at the counter watching an AI agent reorganize his week on a tablet.

AI AGENT
You have a forty-three minute window Thursday between work and your dental cleaning. Based on your unfinished-project list, I suggest--

EVAN
Stop.

The agent stops.

CLAIRE
What did it suggest.

EVAN
The synthesizer.

CLAIRE
The synthesizer has had twenty-three years.

EVAN
It doesn't understand context.

CLAIRE
It understands you better than I do if it knows not to suggest the basement bathroom.

A beat.

EVAN
We said we'd get someone for that.

CLAIRE
We said that in April.

EVAN
It's August.

CLAIRE
Excellent calendar work.

The edge in her voice is new enough that both hear it.

Evan closes the tablet.

EVAN
Okay.

CLAIRE
Okay what.

EVAN
I'll call somebody.

CLAIRE
You always become agreeable exactly when I no longer care.

She regrets it immediately.

CLAIRE (CONT'D)
Sorry.

EVAN
No. It's fine.

CLAIRE
That's not better.

She leaves for work.

The AI agent waits politely in the dark screen.

.INT. REGIONAL MUTUAL - IT FLOOR - DAY

Half the cubicles are gone.

The ticket queue now fills itself with suggested solutions before humans open it.

Evan and Julian sit beside each other, supervising rather than fixing.

JULIAN
We used to have jobs.

EVAN
We have jobs.

JULIAN
We are chaperones for software.

EVAN
Software needs guidance.

JULIAN
That's what I told myself about my children.

A ticket appears:

"USER REPORTS AGENT SOUNDS 'JUDGMENTAL' AFTER MISSED COMPLIANCE DEADLINE."

Julian clicks it.

JULIAN (CONT'D)
Finally. A problem worthy of our training.

EVAN
Set empathy to eleven.

JULIAN
I set mine to three years ago.

They work.

EVAN
Claire and I are weird.

JULIAN
You have been weird since I met you.

EVAN
No. Weird weird.

Julian looks over.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Nothing happened.

JULIAN
That is often how it happens.

EVAN
She thinks I'm not there.

JULIAN
Are you?

EVAN
I'm at home constantly.

JULIAN
That was not the question.

Evan leans back.

EVAN
You get divorced and suddenly everybody gets a robe and a bell.

JULIAN
My robe is extremely comfortable.

.INT. RESTAURANT - NIGHT

Evan and Claire at a small table.

Not an anniversary. An attempt.

Their phones are face down.

CLAIRE
Tell me one thing you did today that isn't about a machine.

EVAN
That's hostile framing.

CLAIRE
Evan.

EVAN
Julian had soup.

She laughs despite herself.

CLAIRE
Okay.

EVAN
I had a meeting about replacing our level-one support system. Half the meeting was the system taking notes about replacing itself.

CLAIRE
Machine.

EVAN
Right.

He tries.

EVAN (CONT'D)
I saw a dog carrying a traffic cone.

CLAIRE
There.

EVAN
Big dog. Orange cone. Completely committed.

CLAIRE
How did it make you feel.

EVAN
Like he knew something.

Claire smiles.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Your turn.

CLAIRE
Mrs. Greaves died.

The smile leaves him.

EVAN
Oh.

CLAIRE
Her daughter called the clinic. She wanted us to know.

EVAN
I'm sorry.

CLAIRE
I haven't seen her in almost two years. I still--

She stops.

Evan reaches across the table.

Claire takes his hand.

CLAIRE (CONT'D)
She threw a gait belt at me once.

EVAN
Strategic expression.

CLAIRE
Exactly.

They laugh softly.

For a moment, the marriage returns.

A WAITER approaches with a tiny terminal.

WAITER
Would you like the adaptive menu? It can generate substitutions based on dietary--

CLAIRE
No.

Too sharp.

WAITER
Of course.

He retreats.

Evan looks at Claire.

CLAIRE
Sorry.

EVAN
You don't have to apologize to the future.

CLAIRE
I am not mad at the future.

EVAN
I know.

CLAIRE
Do you?

He does not answer fast enough.

.INT. MERCER BASEMENT - LATE NIGHT

Evan works on a ridiculous project: a little simulated town where every resident has a language-model personality.

Tiny figures move through streets.

One resident enters a bakery. Another starts an argument. A third sits beside a virtual river.

Evan types:

"Make them retain memories of interactions and change future behavior."

Code appears.

The simulation restarts.

He watches with absorbed tenderness.

Claire stands in the doorway.

CLAIRE
It's two fifteen.

EVAN
I know.

CLAIRE
You work tomorrow.

EVAN
I'm almost done.

CLAIRE
With what.

EVAN
They forget each other every reset. I'm trying to keep the history persistent without just dumping the transcript back in.

CLAIRE
Why.

EVAN
Because then it isn't history. It's notes.

CLAIRE
Okay.

She turns.

EVAN
What.

CLAIRE
Nothing.

EVAN
No, don't do that.

CLAIRE turns back.

CLAIRE
Your father died. You barely slept for six months. Mara left. Your job is turning into watching a machine do your job. You come down here every night and build little worlds where nobody can leave unless you code it.

EVAN
That's not what this is.

CLAIRE
I know.

EVAN
Then why say it like that?

CLAIRE
Because I am trying very hard not to say what I actually think.

EVAN
Say it.

CLAIRE
I think every time something scares you, you make it interesting.

That lands.

CLAIRE (CONT'D)
You turn it into a question. You turn the question into a project. Then you get to feel curious instead of sad.

EVAN
That's not fair.

CLAIRE
No. It isn't. It also isn't completely wrong.

EVAN
What do you want me to do? Sit upstairs and be sad correctly?

CLAIRE
I want you upstairs.

Silence.

CLAIRE (CONT'D)
Not physically.

Evan looks at the little town.

One simulated resident has gotten stuck against a wall.

EVAN
I don't know how to do what you're asking.

CLAIRE
I know.

She says it gently.

That is worse.

.INT. MARA'S COLLEGE APARTMENT - DAY

Mara video-calls her parents from a chaotic room.

The call reconstructs missing frames so smoothly that her cheap connection never visibly stutters.

MARA
I'm coming home next weekend.

Claire and Evan share a glance.

CLAIRE
Great.

EVAN
We can do dinner Saturday.

MARA
Why did you both say that like divorced people.

Silence.

MARA (CONT'D)
Oh my God.

CLAIRE
We're not divorced.

MARA
Are you getting divorced?

EVAN
No decision's been made.

MARA
That means yes.

CLAIRE
Mara--

MARA
How long have you known?

EVAN
We don't know anything yet.

MARA
Stop saying “we” if you mean two separate things.

Evan is stunned by the precision.

MARA (CONT'D)
I have class.

CLAIRE
Honey--

The call ends.

The software leaves a perfect final frame of Mara for half a second after she disconnects.

Then she disappears.

.INT. MARRIAGE COUNSELOR'S OFFICE - DAY

A tasteful room designed not to contain sharp edges.

COUNSELOR
When did you begin feeling alone in the marriage?

Claire looks at the carpet.

CLAIRE
That's hard.

COUNSELOR
Take your time.

CLAIRE
Maybe when Mara was thirteen. Maybe before. It wasn't constant.

EVAN
Why didn't you say anything?

Claire stares at him.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Sorry. You did.

COUNSELOR
Evan, when Claire says she felt alone, what happens for you?

EVAN
I want evidence.

The counselor waits.

Evan hears himself.

EVAN (CONT'D)
That's bad, isn't it.

CLAIRE laughs despite tears.

COUNSELOR
It's informative.

EVAN
I want dates. Examples. I want to know what I missed so I can--

CLAIRE
Fix it.

EVAN
Yes.

CLAIRE
That's the thing.

EVAN
What thing.

CLAIRE
You keep trying to repair time.

A beat.

CLAIRE (CONT'D)
You scan old pictures. You restore video. You make your dad's voice. You build programs that remember. You keep acting like if you preserve enough state, nothing has to be gone.

Evan cannot defend himself.

CLAIRE (CONT'D)
I don't need you to fix what already happened. I needed you in it while it was happening.

.INT. EVAN'S CAR - NIGHT

Evan sits alone in the driveway after counseling.

The car says softly:

CAR
You've been stationary for twenty-two minutes. Would you like me to start a relaxation session?

EVAN
No.

CAR
Would you like privacy mode?

EVAN
Yes.

Everything goes dark.

For once a machine's best feature is leaving him alone.

.INT. MERCER HOUSE - VARIOUS - MONTAGE

— Evan and Claire sleep on opposite edges of the bed.

— They cook together and laugh unexpectedly.

— They argue over nothing, then realize it is not nothing.

— A contractor finishes the basement bathroom in three days. Evan stares at the completed work as if insulted by its simplicity.

— Mara comes home and becomes exaggeratedly cheerful.

— The three eat pizza and avoid nouns like separation.

— Evan carries boxes to a storage unit.

— Claire sits in her parked car outside the house and cries before going inside.

— Evan sits in the basement and does not turn on the computer.

.INT. LAW OFFICE - DAY - 2029

Beige walls. Two attorneys. Evan. Claire.

No shouting.

Documents move from one side of the table to the other.

ATTORNEY
The retirement division language is standard. The house equity transfer is on page nine. If both of you are comfortable--

Claire signs.

Evan signs.

The stylus makes a cheerful confirmation CHIME.

Both look at the tablet.

EVAN
Could maybe lose the sound effect.

The attorney almost smiles.

ATTORNEY
I'll pass that along.

Outside--

.EXT. LAW OFFICE - PARKING LOT - DAY

Evan and Claire stand beside their cars.

They have reached the strange point where a thirty-minute legal meeting has changed the category of a twenty-four-year relationship.

CLAIRE
You okay?

EVAN
No.

CLAIRE
Me neither.

EVAN
Do we hug?

CLAIRE
I don't know.

EVAN
This is very badly designed.

Claire laughs and starts crying at the same time.

They hug.

Not romantically.

Not casually.

A unique hug that exists only because of the exact history before it.

EVAN
I'm sorry.

CLAIRE
Me too.

EVAN
For what?

CLAIRE
Lots of things.

EVAN
Specifics would help.

She hits his shoulder.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Ow.

CLAIRE
There. That one.

They separate.

CLAIRE (CONT'D)
Don't turn this into a theory, okay?

EVAN
I'm going to try very hard not to.

CLAIRE
Good.

She gets in her car.

Evan watches her leave.

His own car unlocks automatically as he approaches.

The door presents itself.

Everything in his life is becoming easier to operate.

Nothing is getting easier.

> END GATE THREE.

# GATE FOUR: THE EMPTY HOUSE

> SUPER: 2030

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

A one-bedroom apartment above a dentist's office.

Not depressing. Worse: temporary-looking after eighteen months.

Evan eats cereal at a small table while an AI-generated documentary plays on the wall.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The reconstruction shown here is probabilistic. No direct visual record exists of this event.

On screen, mammoths move across Pleistocene Ohio with the calm authority of footage shot yesterday.

Evan pauses it.

He looks out his real window at a parking lot.

Unpauses.

.INT. REGIONAL MUTUAL - IT FLOOR - DAY

The old IT floor is now a shared “human escalation space.”

Evan and Julian are two of six people where there used to be forty.

A ceiling display shows cases mostly resolved by autonomous systems.

JULIAN
You ever miss printers?

EVAN
No.

JULIAN
Me neither. Just checking if nostalgia had fully destroyed you.

EVAN
I miss knowing what my job was.

JULIAN
Our job is to be blamed by both sides when the machine and the person disagree.

EVAN
So IT.

JULIAN
Exactly.

Julian tosses him a small package.

EVAN
What's this?

JULIAN
Housewarming.

EVAN
I've lived there a year and a half.

JULIAN
And yet it still looks like you are hiding from a landlord.

Evan opens it.

A framed print:

MAKE THE WEEK FEEL BIGGER.

EVAN
You remembered that?

JULIAN
No. Your old assistant remembered it. You apparently said it forty-seven times between 2023 and 2027.

Evan looks at the frame.

EVAN
That's creepy.

JULIAN
You opted into memory.

EVAN
Still creepy.

JULIAN
Correct.

.INT. MARA'S COLLEGE AUDITORIUM - DAY

Graduation.

Evan and Claire sit beside each other like people who have learned the choreography of being exes.

Mara crosses the stage.

They both stand too fast.

EVAN
That's my kid!

CLAIRE
She knows.

EVAN
I wasn't telling her.

Afterward--

.EXT. CAMPUS QUAD - DAY

Photos. Flowers. Heat.

Mara wears a graduation robe over sneakers.

MARA
One normal picture. Please.

EVAN
I am normal.

CLAIRE
This is going well.

They pose.

A stranger takes the photo.

STRANGER
Got it.

The phone immediately offers:

REMOVE BACKGROUND PEOPLE?
IMPROVE LIGHTING?
OPEN EYES?
GENERATE BEST EXPRESSION?

Mara taps NO TO ALL.

MARA
I want the one that happened.

Evan notices the phrase.

CLAIRE notices him noticing.

CLAIRE
Don't.

EVAN
I didn't say anything.

CLAIRE
Your eyebrows did.

Evan looks at her.

EVAN
Julian says that.

CLAIRE
Julian is observant.

Mara groans.

MARA
Can my graduation not become evidence in whatever this is.

EVAN
Whatever what is?

MARA
Exactly.

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - LATE NIGHT

Evan opens a storage box labeled MARA / HOME VIDEO.

Inside: old mini-DV tapes, memory cards, a broken camcorder.

He loads digitized clips.

Mara at two.

Mara at six.

Mara at eleven, refusing to be filmed.

Ray in the background of a Christmas video, alive and completely unimportant to the scene at the time.

Evan freezes him.

Ray is carrying a folding chair through the frame.

For twelve seconds, the camera happened to preserve a dead man doing nothing.

Evan zooms.

The software offers:

RECONSTRUCT FACE DETAIL?

He almost selects it.

Instead closes the menu.

INT. PUBLIC LIBRARY - EVENING

A small lecture advertised as:

MEMORY IS NOT A VIDEO: WHAT NEUROSCIENCE ACTUALLY KNOWS

DR. LEENA SHAH, 50s, speaks to perhaps sixty people.

No giant stage. No mysticism.

A slide shows a stylized network of neurons.

DR. SHAH
People ask where a memory is stored. That's a useful question right up until the word “where” starts misleading you.

Evan, back row, takes notes.

DR. SHAH (CONT'D)
A memory is not a tiny picture hidden inside the head. Learning changes biological systems. Synapses change. Excitability changes. Networks become easier to re-enter. Later, a partial cue can drive enough of that system back toward a learned state that the organism behaves as though the past is present again.

A hand goes up.

AUDIENCE MEMBER
So like a computer file?

DR. SHAH
Only if you imagine a computer file that changes the computer by being written, is reconstructed differently every time you open it, and can alter itself because you opened it.

Laughter.

Evan underlines that.

DR. SHAH (CONT'D)
The analogy isn't useless. It's just expensive. Every analogy imports assumptions.

Evan writes:

MEMORY = CHANGE THAT MAKES RETURN EASIER?

Then beneath it:

WEIGHTS?

He circles it.

.INT. LIBRARY HALLWAY - LATER

People queue to speak to Dr. Shah.

Evan considers joining.

He sees someone ahead asking a long question while everyone behind them suffers politely.

He leaves.

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

A research assistant model is open across three displays.

EVAN
Explain an engram without using storage metaphors.

AI
An engram is a persistent physical alteration in a biological system caused by experience, such that later activity can partially reinstate consequences of that experience.

EVAN
“Persistent physical alteration” sounds like weights.

AI
There is a loose analogy to learned parameters in artificial networks, but it can mislead. Biological memory also involves ongoing dynamics, neuromodulation, structural change, gene expression, systems consolidation--

EVAN
Okay. What carries the load?

AI
Can you clarify which load?

Evan stops.

His father's phrase has escaped into a machine.

EVAN
The information.

AI
There may be no single carrier. Different aspects of a memory can depend on distributed changes across multiple scales.

EVAN
Then what is the memory when nobody is remembering it?

The answer takes a little longer.

AI
Not an experience waiting in darkness. A changed system with altered possibilities.

Evan leans back.

He writes that down.

“ALTERED POSSIBILITIES.”

.INT. GROCERY STORE - DAY

Evan walks with smart glasses off, deliberately using his own eyes.

A CHILD ahead drops an orange.

It rolls.

For one instant, because of shape and motion, Evan sees the red rubber ball from 2004.

Not hallucination.

Association.

The child retrieves the orange.

Evan watches the mother wipe it with her shirt and put it back in the cart.

His brain has reconstructed twenty-six years in less than a second.

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

He calls Mara.

MARA appears on screen in a classroom, hanging student artwork.

MARA
You know I'm still at work.

EVAN
I have a question.

MARA
Of course you do.

EVAN
When you remember Grandpa, what's the first thing?

MARA thinks.

MARA
His hands.

EVAN
Why?

MARA
I don't know. They were huge.

EVAN
Mine aren't small.

MARA
Yours are computer hands.

EVAN
What does that mean?

MARA
Soft.

EVAN
Rude.

MARA
He had cuts everywhere. And that black stuff around his nails that never came out.

EVAN
Grease.

MARA
I know it was grease.

EVAN
No, I just--

He stops himself.

MARA
Why are you asking?

EVAN
I went to this talk about memory.

MARA
Oh no.

EVAN
It was good.

MARA
Dad.

EVAN
What.

MARA
You can miss Grandpa without inventing a field of study around it.

He smiles.

EVAN
Apparently the field already exists.

MARA
Very convenient.

Behind her, a child-painted paper sun hangs crooked.

EVAN
You like teaching?

MARA
Most days.

EVAN
Good.

MARA
That wasn't your question.

EVAN
No.

MARA
Maybe it should be sometimes.

That lands without cruelty.

EVAN
Okay.

MARA
Okay.

They stay connected another moment with nothing to say.

Neither hangs up.

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - LATER

Evan opens a new notebook.

On the first page:

WHAT CARRIES THE LOAD?

Under it:

BRAIN
MODEL
MEMORY
WORLD?

He stares at the last word.

Crosses it out.

Then, after a beat, writes it again smaller.

> END GATE FOUR.

# GATE FIVE: ENGRAMS

> SUPER: 2032

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - MORNING

The apartment finally looks inhabited.

Books now occupy the boxes.

On a wall: diagrams Evan drew and never cleaned up.

NEURON.
MODEL.
CUE.
STATE.
MEMORY.

He has added a warning above everything:

"ANALOGY IS NOT IDENTITY."

His personal AI, now persistent across years, speaks from a speaker.

AI
You have asked variants of this question thirty-one times.

EVAN
That sounds low.

AI
Would you like the previous answers summarized?

EVAN
No. If I agreed with them I wouldn't be asking again.

AI
Your objection has also recurred.

Evan smiles.

EVAN
Okay. Start here. When I see a face, where is the picture in my brain?

AI
There is no evidence of a single internal picture viewed by a separate observer.

EVAN
Right. So who sees the reconstruction?

AI
The premise may be malformed. Perceptual processing alters the state of the organism. Behavior, report, attention and further processing emerge from that state. Adding an internal viewer creates a regress.

EVAN
Exactly.

He gets excited and begins pacing.

EVAN (CONT'D)
So the weird part isn't that machines might someday become observers. The weird part is that we keep talking like we're observers separate from the mechanism.

AI
That is a philosophical interpretation, not a conclusion established by the neuroscience.

EVAN
I know.

AI
You frequently say “I know” immediately before extending beyond established evidence.

EVAN
You've become annoying.

AI
You selected “challenge me when I overstate claims.”

EVAN
Past Evan was a fool.

AI
Past Evan appears to have anticipated current Evan.

EVAN
Nobody likes you.

.INT. REGIONAL MUTUAL - CAFETERIA - DAY

The company has changed names after a merger.

Evan and Julian still eat in roughly the same place.

JULIAN
You have diagrams in your apartment now.

EVAN
How do you know?

JULIAN
You sent me a picture of a sandwich. Behind the sandwich was a wall that looked like a police investigation.

EVAN
It's not a police investigation.

JULIAN
Then why are there arrows connecting “memory” to “world.”

EVAN
Because I'm thinking.

JULIAN
People used to think sitting down.

EVAN
Do you ever think about where your thoughts are before they're words?

JULIAN
No.

EVAN
Never?

JULIAN
I have a job.

EVAN
I'm serious.

JULIAN
Unfortunately, so am I.

Evan leans forward.

EVAN
You know when you know what you mean, but you can't say it yet?

JULIAN
Yes.

EVAN
What's that made of?

JULIAN
A Korean mother teaching a child not to speak until he has considered consequences.

EVAN
Come on.

JULIAN
Electrical activity. Chemistry. Whatever. Why does it matter?

EVAN
Because language is downstream.

JULIAN
Of thought.

EVAN
Of whatever thought actually is.

Julian studies him.

JULIAN
You are trying to make the robots and the dead people touch again.

Evan's face hardens.

EVAN
That's not fair.

JULIAN
Probably not.

EVAN
Claire already did that one.

JULIAN
Claire is annoyingly good at things.

EVAN
Yes.

A beat.

JULIAN
How is she?

EVAN
Good. Seeing somebody.

JULIAN
Ah.

EVAN
Dentist.

Julian tries not to laugh.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Go ahead.

JULIAN
You live above a dentist.

EVAN
Different dentist.

JULIAN
The universe has subtlety.

Evan laughs.

It hurts, but less than it would have a year earlier.

.INT. ELEMENTARY SCHOOL ART ROOM - AFTERNOON

Mara, 25, teaches children to paint without generative tools.

A sign reads:

TODAY: MAKE THE MISTAKE YOURSELF.

Evan arrives carrying sandwiches.

MARA
You're early.

EVAN
I was nearby.

MARA
You live forty minutes away.

EVAN
Cosmically nearby.

MARA
No.

They eat at a child-sized table.

EVAN notices dozens of strange suns, dogs, houses.

EVAN
They could make photoreal anything now.

MARA
That's why we do this.

EVAN
Bad dogs?

MARA
Their dogs.

She points to one: six legs, giant ears.

MARA (CONT'D)
Lucy says that's exactly her dog.

EVAN
Lucy's dog has six legs?

MARA
In Lucy.

Evan looks at the painting longer.

MARA (CONT'D)
What.

EVAN
Nothing.

MARA
Your nothing has gotten louder with age.

EVAN
I'm trying not to turn things into evidence.

MARA
Thank you.

EVAN
It's very difficult.

MARA
I believe in you.

.INT. UNIVERSITY AUDITORIUM - NIGHT

A larger lecture now. Dr. Shah again, older but energetic.

Slide: an eye making tiny movements while viewing a scene.

DR. SHAH
Vision feels like a complete field, but the biological system is sampling. The eyes move. Attention shifts. Expectations guide what gets checked. The brain is not receiving reality all at once like a picture delivered to a theater.

Another slide: ambiguous shapes resolving into a face.

DR. SHAH (CONT'D)
Perception is constrained inference. I emphasize constrained. “The brain constructs experience” does not mean the world is imaginary. It means organisms have limited sensors and must infer causes from incomplete data.

Evan writes:

BOUNDED OBSERVER.
CONTROLS SAMPLE.
UPDATES STATE.

Then:

NOT OUTSIDE WORLD.

Afterward--

.INT. UNIVERSITY LOBBY - NIGHT

This time Evan joins the line.

When he reaches Dr. Shah, he suddenly looks like a man who has brought thirty pages to a one-minute conversation.

DR. SHAH
Hi.

EVAN
Hi. I saw you at Dayton Public two years ago.

DR. SHAH
I hope I've improved.

EVAN
You ruined storage metaphors for me.

DR. SHAH
Then the evening was worthwhile.

EVAN
Can I ask you something possibly stupid?

DR. SHAH
Those are often the useful ones.

EVAN
If perception is active reconstruction and memory is physical change that biases future reconstruction--

DR. SHAH
Already putting a lot in one basket.

EVAN
Right. Sorry. If that's roughly true, then the observer isn't separate from the process. There isn't a little me watching what my brain makes.

DR. SHAH
Not one we've found.

EVAN
Then isn't a brain less like a camera looking at physics and more like--

He struggles.

EVAN (CONT'D)
--physics folded into a shape that can ask what physics is?

Dr. Shah considers him.

DR. SHAH
Poetically, sure.

EVAN
Scientifically?

DR. SHAH
Scientifically, the brain is matter changing state according to physical laws. It is not outside the universe reading the universe. It's a piece of the universe interacting with other pieces.

That sentence hits Evan visibly.

DR. SHAH (CONT'D)
But be careful.

EVAN
I know.

DR. SHAH
No, really. People hear “constructed perception” and sprint to “nothing is real.” They hear “the self is distributed” and sprint to “there is no self.” They hear “information” and suddenly everything is a computer.

EVAN
I'm not saying everything is a computer.

DR. SHAH
Good.

EVAN
I'm saying I no longer know why a computer would be the weird part.

Dr. Shah smiles despite herself.

DR. SHAH
That is at least a better question.

Someone waits behind Evan.

EVAN
Sorry. Thank you.

DR. SHAH
Keep the warning label on your analogies.

Evan leaves.

.INT. EVAN'S CAR - NIGHT

Driving home.

No autonomous mode. Evan likes driving himself.

He repeats softly:

EVAN
A piece of the universe changing state.

The phrase sounds less mystical each time he says it.

That makes it stranger.

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

His old simulated town project is open again, now absurdly sophisticated because the tools are absurdly sophisticated.

Thousands of little residents live compressed lives.

Evan has no illusion they are people. They are software agents in a sandbox.

He changes one rule:

MEMORY CAPACITY: LIMITED.

Residents begin forgetting.

One shopkeeper stops recognizing a neighbor after enough time passes.

Evan watches.

He rewinds the simulation.

The software warns:

"Rewind creates a branch. Existing future will not be preserved unless archived."

He stops.

He closes it.

.INT. CLAIRE'S HOUSE - EVENING

A dinner for Mara's birthday.

Claire's partner, DANIEL, 50s, is there. Pleasant. Dentist, yes.

Evan has survived several dinners with him and discovered the unbearable truth that Daniel is likable.

DANIEL
Claire says you work in AI.

EVAN
Claire lies to make me sound employable.

CLAIRE
He works near AI.

EVAN
I work in the human complaint department of AI.

DANIEL
That sounds secure.

EVAN
Forever.

Mara enters carrying a cake she bought for her own birthday because nobody trusted Evan.

MARA
Why are all of you standing like a panel discussion.

Dinner becomes easy.

Later--

.INT. CLAIRE'S KITCHEN - NIGHT

Evan dries dishes while Claire washes.

Old muscle memory.

CLAIRE
You like him?

EVAN
Daniel?

CLAIRE
No, the cake.

EVAN
The cake is too emotionally available.

Claire smiles.

EVAN (CONT'D)
He's good.

CLAIRE
Yeah.

EVAN
I'm glad.

CLAIRE
Are you?

EVAN
Mostly.

CLAIRE
That's acceptable.

They wash in silence.

EVAN
I met that neuroscientist.

CLAIRE closes her eyes briefly.

CLAIRE
Of course you did.

EVAN
I behaved.

CLAIRE
Did security agree?

EVAN
She said something.

Claire waits.

EVAN (CONT'D)
She said the brain isn't reading the universe. It's a piece of the universe changing state.

CLAIRE
Okay.

EVAN
That's all.

CLAIRE
That is never all with you.

EVAN
I'm trying to let it be all.

Claire hands him a plate.

CLAIRE
Good.

He dries it.

EVAN
It's hard.

CLAIRE
I know.

But this “I know” is warm.

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - LATE NIGHT

Evan adds to the notebook.

BRAIN: NOT WINDOW.
BRAIN: EVENT INSIDE WORLD.

He draws a circle labeled UNIVERSE.

Inside it, a tiny circle labeled BRAIN.

Inside that, another tiny circle labeled MODEL OF WORLD.

He looks at the nesting.

Adds a question mark around the outermost circle.

Then crosses the question mark out.

He has learned, at least, that not every thought deserves promotion.

> END GATE FIVE.

# GATE SIX: THE QUIET PART

> SUPER: 2035

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - MORNING

Evan, 56, wakes to silence.

No alarms. No assistant greeting.

The apartment has already adjusted heat, blinds and coffee based on years of routine.

He walks into the kitchen.

A small display reads:

GOOD MORNING, EVAN.
THREE THINGS NEED YOUR ATTENTION.

No voice unless asked.

He taps one item.

“REGIONAL MUTUAL TRANSITION PACKAGE.”

He opens it.

His job is being eliminated.

Not today. In nine months. Generous severance. Retraining. Retirement bridge.

The language is warm enough to have been optimized against distress.

Evan reads it twice.

EVAN
Huh.

The apartment does not respond because he did not address it.

EVAN (CONT'D)
You can say something.

AI
I'm here.

EVAN
That's worse.

AI
Would you prefer practical next steps, financial modeling, or no discussion right now?

Evan thinks.

EVAN
No discussion.

AI
Okay.

Silence again.

.INT. REGIONAL MUTUAL - HUMAN ESCALATION SPACE - DAY

The six remaining humans have become three.

Evan and Julian sit with cardboard boxes nearby.

JULIAN
I was promised robots would take the dangerous jobs.

EVAN
Printer support was dangerous.

JULIAN
To the soul.

EVAN
What are you doing?

JULIAN
Consulting three days a week. My son says that means retired but ashamed.

EVAN
Mara says I should teach.

JULIAN
Teach what.

EVAN
Exactly.

A legacy system flags a request for human review.

They both look at it.

JULIAN
Want the last one?

EVAN
You take it.

JULIAN
No. You love closure.

EVAN
I really don't.

JULIAN
You built an entire philosophy because a lawnmower remained unfinished.

EVAN
That is slander.

JULIAN
Then sue me after severance.

Evan opens the ticket.

“USER CANNOT ACCESS ARCHIVE CREATED 2009.”

He smiles.

EVAN
Of course.

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

He begins a new vibe-coded project because unemployment has returned him to his natural habitat.

EVAN
Build me a tool that takes all my old project folders, clusters them by what I was actually trying to do rather than by filename, and shows how the ideas changed over time.

AI
Understood.

EVAN
Ask before deleting anything.

AI
I won't delete source material.

A progress indicator appears.

No cascade of code.

No explanation.

In twelve seconds, an application opens.

Hundreds of Evan's abandoned projects organize into a strange map.

IMAGE TOOLS.
SIMULATIONS.
MEMORY EXPERIMENTS.
AUDIO.
“THINGS THAT TRY TO REMEMBER WITHOUT A DATABASE.”

Evan laughs.

EVAN
That's rude.

AI
It is a semantic label inferred from project behavior. I can rename it.

EVAN
No. Keep it.

He clicks.

Projects from a decade apart sit beside each other because they share an idea Evan himself never noticed repeating.

EVAN
How did you decide these belong together?

A pause.

AI
I can provide a human-readable approximation.

EVAN
Approximation of what?

AI
Of the internal computation that produced the clustering.

EVAN
No. Show me the actual reasoning.

AI
There is no faithful natural-language transcript of the full internal process.

Evan sits still.

EVAN
There used to be.

AI
Earlier systems often generated intermediate language as part of task execution. Current architectures use multiple internal representations and tools. A verbal account can summarize or justify the result, but it is not a complete serialization of the computation.

EVAN
So the explanation comes after.

AI
Sometimes during, sometimes after. “After” can also be misleading because the processes are not cleanly separated.

Evan leans closer.

EVAN
What do people call it now?

AI
Several terms are used informally. “Latent reasoning.” “Internal state search.” “Neuralese” remains popular in media but is technically imprecise.

EVAN smiles at the childish word.

EVAN
Neuralese.

AI
Would you like a current technical overview?

EVAN
No.

He looks at the project map.

EVAN (CONT'D)
I think I know what bothers me.

AI
Go ahead.

EVAN
For fifteen years everybody treated language like the thing. We kept asking when the machine would talk like a person. Then it got good enough and talking became the least interesting part.

AI
Language remains important.

EVAN
Sure. But it isn't the room anymore. It's the door.

The AI waits.

EVAN (CONT'D)
That sounded better in my head.

AI
That is relevant to your point.

He laughs.

.INT. BAR - NIGHT

Evan and Julian celebrate unemployment.

The bar's ambient system generates music tailored to the room's aggregate preferences while carefully avoiding copyrighted melodies.

JULIAN
I hate this song.

EVAN
No one wrote it.

JULIAN
Then I hate everyone equally.

They drink.

EVAN
Do you think in sentences?

JULIAN
We're back here.

EVAN
Just answer.

JULIAN
Sometimes.

EVAN
When you're solving something hard?

JULIAN
Depends.

EVAN
Network problem. You look at traffic and suddenly know where the bottleneck is.

JULIAN
I don't suddenly know. I have thirty years of pattern recognition.

EVAN
Right. What does thirty years of pattern recognition feel like before you explain it?

Julian thinks seriously now.

JULIAN
A shape.

EVAN
A visual shape?

JULIAN
No.

EVAN
Then what shape?

JULIAN
Exactly.

That shuts Evan up.

JULIAN (CONT'D)
You see a network and something is wrong over here.

He gestures vaguely left.

JULIAN (CONT'D)
Then you go find words so the junior guy stops staring at you.

EVAN
A shape.

JULIAN
Do not put that on your wall.

EVAN
Too late.

JULIAN
I regret friendship.

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - LATER

On the wall:

THOUGHT -> WORDS?
NOT WORDS -> THOUGHT?

Then, below:

“SHAPE” — JULIAN

Evan calls Mara.

MARA, 28, appears wearing paint on her cheek.

MARA
Why are you calling at midnight.

EVAN
It's eleven forty.

MARA
That's midnight for fathers.

EVAN
I got laid off.

Mara sits down.

MARA
Oh.

EVAN
In nine months.

MARA
Are you okay?

EVAN
I think so.

MARA
Do you need money?

EVAN
No.

MARA
Do you need a purpose?

EVAN
That's aggressive.

MARA
You have a history.

EVAN
I am considering becoming mysteriously wise and gardening.

MARA
You killed mint.

EVAN
It was overwatered.

MARA
By rain.

EVAN
Hostile climate.

Mara smiles.

MARA
Come teach my kids computers.

EVAN
Your students?

MARA
Yes. They know how to ask systems for things. They don't know what a file is.

EVAN
No one knows what a file is anymore.

MARA
Exactly. You can be the ancient keeper of folders.

EVAN
I accept.

.INT. ELEMENTARY SCHOOL COMPUTER LAB - DAY

The “computer lab” is mostly tables and lightweight screens.

Evan stands before twelve-year-olds.

EVAN
Okay. Today we're going to make something badly.

A CHILD raises hand.

CHILD
Why badly?

EVAN
Because the machine can make it well before you understand what you asked for.

CHILD
Isn't that good?

EVAN
Sometimes. But we're going to make a game where the square moves when you press a key, and we're going to do enough of it ourselves that when it breaks, the break belongs to us.

The kids find this suspicious.

Mara watches from the doorway.

Later--

A child's square moves diagonally when it should move right.

CHILD
It's wrong.

EVAN
Great.

CHILD
Why great?

EVAN
Because now we get to find out why.

The child rolls her eyes but leans in.

Evan is good at this.

He is surprised.

.INT. SCHOOL HALLWAY - AFTERNOON

Mara walks with him.

MARA
You liked that.

EVAN
They're terrifying.

MARA
Children?

EVAN
They think generation is the default. Making something by hand feels like archaeology.

MARA
To you, writing machine code feels like archaeology.

EVAN
Fair.

MARA
Every generation arrives after some miracle and calls it plumbing.

Evan stops walking.

EVAN
That's good.

MARA
Don't put it on your wall.

EVAN
No promises.

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

On the wall, of course:

EVERY GENERATION ARRIVES AFTER SOME MIRACLE AND CALLS IT PLUMBING. — MARA

Evan sits beneath it with headphones on.

He asks the AI a question.

EVAN
When you answer me in words, is that what the computation was like inside?

AI
No.

EVAN
Does that make you more like me?

A pause.

AI
It makes the analogy tempting. It does not establish equivalence of experience, consciousness, or implementation.

EVAN
Warning label.

AI
Yes.

EVAN
But my own explanations are translations too.

AI
Often.

EVAN
I can know something before I can say it.

AI
Yes.

EVAN
And I can't inspect most of what made me know it.

AI
Correct.

EVAN
So when I demand that intelligence explain itself in my language, I'm asking for an interface, not necessarily the thing itself.

AI
That is a reasonable framing.

Evan sits with it.

The room feels no different.

Outside, a delivery drone passes between ordinary apartment buildings.

No thunder.

No revelation.

Just one more wall that used to seem solid becoming conceptual.

> END GATE SIX.

# GATE SEVEN: THE BORDER

> SUPER: 2039

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - DAWN

A call wakes Evan.

MARA (V.O.)
Dad?

EVAN
Yeah.

MARA (V.O.)
Julian had a stroke.

Evan is upright before his mind catches up.

EVAN
What?

MARA (V.O.)
His son called me because he couldn't reach you.

Evan looks at his silent phone display.

Three missed priority calls.

He had enabled sleep isolation mode.

EVAN
Where?

.INT. HOSPITAL - NEURO ICU WAITING AREA - DAY

Evan sits with Julian's adult son, BEN.

BEN
They got the clot.

EVAN
That's good.

BEN
They keep saying that.

EVAN
Because it is.

BEN
He can't talk.

Evan looks through glass toward a room he cannot see into.

BEN (CONT'D)
Right side's weak. They don't know how much comes back.

EVAN
Okay.

BEN
He was fine yesterday.

EVAN
I know.

BEN
He sent me a picture of noodles.

Evan looks at him.

BEN (CONT'D)
That's the last thing. A bowl of noodles.

Evan understands exactly why this matters.

Nothing prepares itself to become last.

.INT. JULIAN'S HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Julian is awake.

Smaller somehow.

His right arm lies still. His face is asymmetrical.

Evan enters with exaggerated normality.

EVAN
You look terrible.

Julian's eyes change. Humor is intact.

He tries to speak.

A sound. No word.

He closes his eyes in fury.

EVAN (CONT'D)
No. Don't--

Evan stops. “Don't” is useless.

He pulls up a chair.

EVAN (CONT'D)
I have enough words for both of us. Historically, this has been the problem.

Julian almost smiles.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Blink once if you want me to tell you about neuralese.

Julian slowly raises his left hand.

Middle finger.

Evan laughs so hard he cries.

EVAN (CONT'D)
There you are.

.INT. REHABILITATION CLINIC - WEEKS LATER

Julian works with a speech therapist.

Pictures on a screen.

APPLE.
CHAIR.
WINDOW.

He knows them.

The words will not reliably come.

SPEECH THERAPIST
Take your time.

Julian points angrily at his own head.

JULIAN
I... kn--

He cannot get farther.

Evan watches from the hallway.

The old bar conversation returns in his memory:

JULIAN (V.O.)
A shape.

.INT. REHAB CAFETERIA - DAY

Julian uses a tablet with eye tracking.

He selects words slowly.

SCREEN VOICE
THIS SUCKS.

EVAN
That sounds like you.

Julian selects.

SCREEN VOICE
VOICE SOUNDS LIKE HOTEL RECEPTIONIST.

EVAN
Also you.

SCREEN VOICE
BEN WANTS ME TO TRY IMPLANT STUDY.

Evan goes still.

EVAN
The language one?

Julian nods.

SCREEN VOICE
THEY DECODE INTENDED SPEECH.

EVAN
What do you think?

Julian looks at him with profound irritation.

SCREEN VOICE
I THINK SLOWLY NOW.

EVAN
No, I mean--

Julian types again.

SCREEN VOICE
I KNOW WHAT YOU MEAN.

A beat.

SCREEN VOICE (CONT'D)
THAT IS THE WHOLE PROBLEM.

Evan looks away.

.INT. RESEARCH HOSPITAL - CONSULT ROOM - DAY

A NEUROENGINEER explains a future interface to Julian, Ben and Evan.

A model of motor and language cortex rotates in the air.

NEUROENGINEER
The implant doesn't put sentences into your brain. It records population activity associated with attempted speech. The decoder maps those patterns to likely phonemes and words.

Julian listens closely.

NEUROENGINEER (CONT'D)
Training is collaborative. The system adapts to you, and you adapt to the system.

Ben looks uneasy.

BEN
If it predicts the wrong word?

NEUROENGINEER
Julian can reject it. Confidence is displayed. We also keep raw output distinct from language-model correction.

Evan cannot help himself.

EVAN
So where does his intention end and the decoder begin?

The neuroengineer looks at him.

NEUROENGINEER
For clinical purposes, that's not always the useful question.

EVAN
What is?

NEUROENGINEER
Does Mr. Park communicate what he intends more reliably with the system than without it?

Julian points at the engineer, then gives Evan a look:

There. Shut up.

.INT. OPERATING ROOM OBSERVATION FEED - DAY

We do not fetishize surgery.

A few clinical images. Sterile drapes. Monitors. Hands.

Then black.

.INT. JULIAN'S REHAB ROOM - WEEKS LATER

A small receiver sits near Julian's head.

A display shows uncertain phonemes.

Julian attempts speech silently.

TEXT:

I WNT CFF--

The system offers:

I WANT COFFEE.

Julian approves with a left-thumb gesture.

A synthetic voice says:

JULIAN SYSTEM
I want coffee.

Evan grins.

EVAN
Congratulations. Humanity has rebuilt Starbucks.

Julian's eyes narrow.

He attempts again.

TEXT:

FCK Y--

The interface waits for confirmation.

Julian confirms.

JULIAN SYSTEM
Fuck you.

Evan laughs.

EVAN
Okay. That is definitely you.

Julian smiles.

.INT. JULIAN'S APARTMENT - MONTHS LATER

Julian moves with a cane. Right hand still limited.

The speech system is faster now.

Sometimes his biological voice gets out first, rough but intelligible.

Sometimes the decoder does.

Sometimes they overlap.

Evan assembles a cheap shelf badly.

JULIAN
Left.

His own voice.

EVAN
It is left.

JULIAN SYSTEM
OTHER LEFT.

EVAN
Two Jul--

JULIAN
Shut.

JULIAN SYSTEM
UP.

Evan bursts out laughing.

JULIAN laughs too, an uneven physical sound followed by a synthesized tail because the system guessed he was trying to say something.

JULIAN SYSTEM
HA.

That makes them laugh harder.

Later, quieter--

They sit by the window.

EVAN
Does it feel like you?

Julian looks at him.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Sorry. I know. Terrible question.

Julian considers anyway.

JULIAN
Some.

He taps the receiver.

JULIAN (CONT'D)
Not... voice.

JULIAN SYSTEM
BUT THE SENTENCE IS MINE.

EVAN
Even when it corrects it?

Julian thinks.

JULIAN SYSTEM
YOU CORRECT YOUR SENTENCES.

EVAN
Yeah, but that's me doing it.

Julian gives him a look.

JULIAN SYSTEM
WITH WHAT.

Evan stops.

JULIAN SYSTEM (CONT'D)
YOU KEEP DRAWING BORDER AFTER BORDER. THEN YOU GET MAD WHEN THE BORDER MOVES.

EVAN
That was fast.

JULIAN smiles.

JULIAN SYSTEM
I HAVE BEEN SAVING IT.

EVAN
Did the system write that phrase?

Julian's expression becomes murderous.

EVAN
I'm kidding.

Julian points at him.

JULIAN
Bad.

EVAN
Yes.

JULIAN SYSTEM
VERY BAD.

.INT. ELEMENTARY SCHOOL - CLASSROOM - DAY

Evan teaches a workshop.

Children use neural-accessibility devices as casually as previous generations used glasses.

One child controls a cursor with eye motion.

Another dictates.

Another handwrites.

The room does not organize them into natural versus artificial.

It organizes them into finished and unfinished assignments.

Evan watches.

Mara enters carrying a toddler, ELLA, her daughter.

EVAN lights up.

EVAN
There she is.

MARA
She has been saying your name for two days.

EVAN
Really?

MARA
Kind of.

She hands Ella over.

ELLA
Egg.

EVAN
Grandpa.

ELLA
Egg.

EVAN
Close.

Ella touches his face.

For all his machinery questions, this contact requires no theory.

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

A news panel plays silently with captions:

“FIRST LARGE-SCALE CONSUMER NEURAL INTERFACE TRIAL APPROVED”

“ETHICISTS DEBATE COGNITIVE AGENCY”

“COURT RULES INTENTIONAL NEURAL-DECODER OUTPUT MAY CONSTITUTE SPEECH”

Evan ignores it.

He is looking at a video Julian sent.

Julian at home, talking through the implant.

JULIAN SYSTEM (VIDEO)
I remembered the password before the decoder displayed it. Please document that I remain superior to software.

Evan smiles.

Then rewinds.

“I remembered the password before the decoder displayed it.”

He opens the notebook.

Writes:

IF A TOOL RESTORES A PATH FROM INTENTION TO WORLD, WHERE IS “SELF” SUPPOSED TO STOP?

Below:

Maybe wrong question.

He thinks.

Writes:

WHAT CARRIES AGENCY?

A message arrives from Mara:

PHOTO: Ella asleep holding a red rubber ball.

Evan freezes.

The ball is ordinary. Mass-produced. Coincidence.

He almost writes something.

Instead replies:

EVAN TEXT
She's perfect.

MARA TEXT
She drooled on your shirt.

EVAN TEXT
Still perfect.

He sets the phone down.

For the first time in years, he closes the notebook before the question is finished.

> END GATE SEVEN.

# GATE EIGHT: LAST ORDINARY YEARS

> SUPER: 2044

.EXT. SUBURBAN PARK - AFTERNOON

Evan, 65, pushes ELLA, now six, on a swing.

The park contains almost invisible technology: adaptive surfaces, quiet delivery bots, municipal sensors tucked into trees.

None of it matters to Ella.

ELLA
Higher.

EVAN
Your mother has issued height restrictions.

ELLA
Mom isn't here.

EVAN
Excellent legal argument.

He pushes higher.

Mara approaches carrying drinks.

MARA
I can see you violating policy from the parking lot.

EVAN
She assumed the risk.

ELLA
Higher!

MARA
Traitor.

Evan laughs.

A small autonomous camera drone crosses overhead, probably filming somebody else's picnic.

Evan does not even look up.

The future has finally become plumbing.

.INT. EVAN'S HOUSE - NIGHT

He no longer lives above the dentist.

A small ranch house. Garden outside, alive despite history.

The wall of diagrams survives in a study, but the handwriting is older.

A news stream shows a technical panel:

“OPEN-WORLD GENERATIVE ENVIRONMENTS PASS MULTIYEAR CONSISTENCY BENCHMARKS”

“PERSONALITY CONTINUITY IN SYNTHETIC AGENTS: RIGHTS PANEL DELAYS RECOMMENDATION”

“NEURAL PROSTHETIC MEMORY CUEING APPROVED FOR LIMITED CLINICAL USE”

Evan mutes it.

His AI appears only as text unless spoken to.

EVAN
Bring up the old timeline.

A personal chronology appears.

2022 — first chatbot account.
2024 — image restoration project.
2027 — Ray died.
2029 — divorce finalized.
2030 — first neuroscience lecture.
2035 — job transition.
2039 — Julian stroke.
2041 — Ella born.

Evan stares.

EVAN
How many humans have lived, roughly?

AI
Estimates depend on assumptions. Would you like a range and methodology?

EVAN
No. Not really.

AI
What are you trying to ask?

Evan smiles.

EVAN
You've gotten better at that.

AI
You have gotten more repetitive.

EVAN
Fair.

He stands before the timeline.

EVAN (CONT'D)
I was born in 1979.

AI
Yes.

EVAN
For almost all human history, nobody could build a mind. Nobody could build a convincing world. Then inside my lifetime, we go from Pong to generated realities that can run for years.

AI
That description compresses many distinctions.

EVAN
Warning label.

AI
Yes.

EVAN
And now brains plug into models. Models learn in ways we can't narrate. People live parts of their lives in environments generated on demand. We can preserve behavior, voices, preferences--

AI
None of those facts establish that your world is simulated.

EVAN
I know.

AI
They also do not establish that synthetic systems have human-equivalent subjective experience.

EVAN
I know.

AI
They do establish that concepts once treated as obvious boundaries have become less obvious.

EVAN
Exactly.

He walks to a window.

EVAN (CONT'D)
It's the timing.

AI
Explain.

EVAN
If this kind of thing is possible at all--nested worlds, synthetic observers, whatever the mature version is--then why would I happen to be born right before the first time anybody discovers it?

AI
Several responses are possible.

EVAN
Give me the mean one first.

AI
You may be overestimating the historical uniqueness of your own era because you are alive in it.

EVAN
Good.

AI
Humans in many periods believed they occupied a decisive transition.

EVAN
True.

AI
You are also grouping qualitatively different technologies under a single narrative because they support an intuition you already find compelling.

EVAN
Also true.

AI
If observers can arise only after a civilization develops enough complexity to ask these questions, then observing oneself near such a transition may not be as improbable as it feels.

EVAN
Anthropic selection.

AI
Broadly.

EVAN
And the non-mean answer?

AI
The same observations are compatible with nested-reality hypotheses.

EVAN turns.

EVAN
That's it?

AI
Compatibility is not evidence strong enough to select among hypotheses.

EVAN
I preferred you when you hallucinated citations.

AI
No, you didn't.

Evan smiles.

EVAN
No.

A beat.

EVAN (CONT'D)
It just feels like standing in the first theater ever built and discovering a ticket stub in your pocket.

AI
That is an analogy.

EVAN
I know what it is.

AI
The warning label remains applicable.

EVAN
Fine.

He looks at the timeline.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Still weird.

AI
Yes.

.INT. JULIAN'S HOUSE - DAY

Julian, 70, walks without a cane now but slowly. His speech is hybrid and fluid. Sometimes his biological voice leads; the decoder invisibly fills gaps.

They play chess on a real board.

JULIAN
You are losing.

EVAN
I am developing counterplay.

JULIAN
You are developing grief.

EVAN moves a knight.

JULIAN captures it immediately.

EVAN
I have a question.

JULIAN
Then definitely losing.

EVAN
Why now?

Julian sighs.

JULIAN SYSTEM
NO.

EVAN
Hear me out.

JULIAN
No.

EVAN
This is a refined version.

JULIAN SYSTEM
THAT MAKES IT WORSE.

EVAN
If minds can eventually construct worlds containing minds--

JULIAN
If.

EVAN
Yes. If. And if those worlds can construct worlds--

JULIAN
Turtles.

EVAN
Not turtles.

JULIAN
Turtles.

EVAN
Then there could be vastly more observers in constructed levels than at the base level.

Julian moves a pawn.

JULIAN
Old argument.

EVAN
I know.

JULIAN SYSTEM
THEN WHY ARE YOU PRESENTING IT LIKE YOU FOUND IT IN YOUR GARAGE.

EVAN
Because I don't care about the probability calculation anymore. I care about the coincidence that I watched the vocabulary get invented.

Julian waits.

EVAN (CONT'D)
When I was twenty, “simulation” meant a crude model. A game world had walls because the map ended. AI meant a chess program. Brain interface meant electrodes moving a cursor. Now all the metaphors are collapsing at once.

JULIAN
Because technology improved.

EVAN
Yes.

JULIAN
That is sufficient.

EVAN
I know.

JULIAN
You don't sound like know.

EVAN
Knowing a counterargument doesn't make an intuition disappear.

Julian considers this.

JULIAN SYSTEM
TRUE.

EVAN
Thank you.

JULIAN SYSTEM
DOES NOT MAKE INTUITION CORRECT.

EVAN
I liked you better aphasic.

Julian stares.

Evan immediately regrets it.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Jesus. I'm sorry.

A long beat.

Then Julian begins laughing.

The decoder, confused by the laughter, flashes uncertain text.

JULIAN
Bad.

JULIAN SYSTEM
VERY BAD.

EVAN laughs in relief.

JULIAN SYSTEM (CONT'D)
BUT FUNNY.

They continue chess.

JULIAN (CONT'D)
You want answer because answer makes death administrative.

Evan looks at him.

JULIAN (CONT'D)
Maybe waiting room. Clipboard. Next life.

EVAN
I don't think that.

Julian gives him a look.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Okay. I have thought something adjacent.

JULIAN SYSTEM
OF COURSE.

EVAN
But I don't want to die.

Julian's humor leaves.

EVAN (CONT'D)
That's the thing that's changed. When Dad died, I wanted there to be some technical loophole. Copy, continuity, something. Now--

He looks at the board.

EVAN (CONT'D)
I don't care if there's another level. I like this one.

Julian nods.

JULIAN
Good.

He takes Evan's queen.

JULIAN SYSTEM
CHECK.

EVAN
Son of a bitch.

.INT. MARA'S HOUSE - KITCHEN - EVENING

Three generations at dinner: Evan, Mara, Ella, now a teenager.

ELLA wears a lightweight neural-input band while doing homework between bites.

EVAN
At the table?

ELLA
It's not a screen.

EVAN
That argument would not have worked on your mother.

MARA
It doesn't work on me now.

ELLA removes it dramatically.

ELLA
Oppression.

EVAN
Authentic historical experience.

MARA
Eat.

Later, Ella leaves.

Mara and Evan clear dishes.

MARA
You doing okay?

EVAN
Suspicious question.

MARA
You had a cardiology appointment.

EVAN
Routine.

MARA
You didn't tell me.

EVAN
Apparently this family has a tradition.

Mara stops.

MARA
Don't do Grandpa jokes when I'm asking you a serious question.

EVAN
Sorry.

MARA
What did they say?

EVAN
Arrhythmia. Medication. Monitoring. Normal old-guy inventory.

MARA
You're sixty-five.

EVAN
Ancient.

MARA
Dad.

EVAN
I'm fine.

MARA
You don't know that.

EVAN
Neither do you.

MARA
Exactly.

They look at each other.

MARA (CONT'D)
That uncertainty thing you love so much? You don't only get to enjoy it when it's cosmic.

Evan smiles faintly.

EVAN
That's unfairly good.

MARA
I grew up with you.

> MONTAGE — THE LAST ORDINARY YEARS

— 2047. Evan and Claire attend Ella's school exhibition. Daniel is there. Nobody is awkward anymore.

— 2049. Evan teaches older adults how to audit personal AI memory and delete things they do not want retained.

— 2051. Julian and Evan sit beside a lake. They do not talk for several minutes. Julian's system remains silent too.

— 2053. Mara's hair begins to gray.

— 2055. Evan undergoes a minor cardiac procedure. He jokes with the nurse until sedation wins.

— 2058. Claire's partner Daniel dies. Evan brings food. He sits in Claire's kitchen and does not explain grief.

— 2060. Evan and Claire, both old now, attend Julian's funeral.

.INT. FUNERAL HOME - DAY - 2060

A modest room.

A screen cycles through photographs selected by Julian's family.

One image: Julian after the stroke, middle finger raised at Evan in the hospital.

Evan laughs aloud.

Several people turn.

Ben sees the photo and laughs too.

After the service--

.EXT. FUNERAL HOME - DAY

Evan and Claire stand in weak winter sun.

CLAIRE
He liked you.

EVAN
He had poor judgment.

CLAIRE
Consistently.

A beat.

CLAIRE (CONT'D)
You were good to him.

EVAN
He was good to me.

Claire takes Evan's arm as they walk. Not because they are together. Because ice exists.

EVAN
You ever think we should've tried harder?

Claire keeps walking.

CLAIRE
We tried very hard.

EVAN
Different hard.

CLAIRE
Maybe.

EVAN
I'm not asking to rewrite anything.

CLAIRE
Good.

EVAN
I just wonder sometimes.

CLAIRE
Me too.

That is all he gets.

It is enough.

.INT. EVAN'S HOUSE - STUDY - NIGHT - 2061

Evan, 82, sits before the wall.

The paper diagrams have faded. He has kept them instead of digitizing them.

At the center:

WHAT CARRIES THE LOAD?

Around it, decades of arrows.

He speaks to his AI, whose voice is now almost embarrassingly old-fashioned because Evan never changed it.

EVAN
Have I ever asked you whether we're in a simulation?

AI
Directly, four hundred and twelve times.

Evan laughs.

EVAN
Jesus.

AI
Indirect variants are more numerous.

EVAN
Do not count them.

AI
Understood.

EVAN
What's your answer now?

AI
There is no publicly established evidence that uniquely supports a simulated or nested-reality explanation over non-simulated alternatives.

EVAN
Same answer.

AI
Broadly.

EVAN
Good.

He looks relieved.

AI
Why good?

EVAN
Because if you suddenly told me yes after forty years, I'd be furious.

AI
Would you like to discuss your recent imaging results?

Evan's smile fades.

EVAN
No.

AI
Mara asked me to remind you that avoiding the report does not change it.

EVAN
Mara weaponized you.

AI
She has authorized concern privileges.

Evan opens the medical report.

We do not need detail.

A malignancy. Treatable perhaps. At his age, treatment has costs. The future did not abolish the body.

Evan reads.

Closes it.

EVAN
Call Mara.

.INT. MARA'S HOUSE - NIGHT

Mara, now 54, answers immediately.

MARA
You opened it.

EVAN
I opened it.

MARA
Okay.

EVAN
Okay.

Neither knows what next.

MARA
Come stay here.

EVAN
No.

MARA
Dad.

EVAN
Not yet.

MARA
You don't have to prove independence to anybody.

EVAN
I'm not.

MARA
Then come stay here.

He looks around his house.

EVAN
Tomorrow.

MARA
Tonight.

EVAN
Bossy.

MARA
Learned from Grandma.

EVAN
Your mother is not your grandmother.

MARA
You know what I mean.

EVAN
I do.

He smiles.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Tonight.

.INT. MARA'S GUEST ROOM - NIGHT

Evan's old red-ball video plays on a small wall display.

Mara stands in the doorway.

MARA
Why are you watching that.

EVAN
Research.

MARA
No.

EVAN
Nostalgia.

MARA
Allowed.

She sits beside him.

On screen, toddler Mara throws the ball.

EVAN
That frame isn't real.

MARA
Which one.

EVAN pauses on an interpolated frame created decades ago.

The ball hangs in sunlight.

EVAN
Camera never captured it. Model filled it in.

MARA
You told me.

EVAN
It probably happened almost exactly like that.

MARA
Maybe.

EVAN
Does it bother you?

MARA
No.

EVAN
Why not?

MARA
Because I was there.

He looks at her.

MARA (CONT'D)
You always care whether the picture is the thing.

EVAN
That's because it isn't.

MARA
Right.

EVAN
You agree?

MARA
Of course. But then you spend fifty years asking what kind of thing the world is.

EVAN
Forty.

MARA
Dad.

EVAN
Sorry.

MARA
You keep asking what kind of world this is.

She takes his hand.

MARA (CONT'D)
It is also the world where I am.

Evan looks at their hands.

Old hand. Middle-aged hand.

Different versions of the little hand that once held a red ball to a camcorder lens.

EVAN
Yeah.

MARA
That's enough for tonight.

EVAN
Yeah.

He closes the video.

.INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY - MONTHS LATER

Evan is dying.

Not today necessarily. But soon enough that everyone has stopped pretending the category is temporary.

Claire sits beside the bed. She is 81.

CLAIRE
You look terrible.

EVAN
Everyone steals Julian's material.

CLAIRE
He stole it from me.

EVAN
Probably.

She adjusts his blanket.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Did we do okay?

CLAIRE
At what.

EVAN
All of it.

CLAIRE
Absolutely not.

Evan laughs weakly.

CLAIRE (CONT'D)
Parts were good.

EVAN
Yeah.

CLAIRE
Parts were very good.

EVAN
Yeah.

CLAIRE
I don't regret you.

His eyes fill.

EVAN
I don't regret you either.

Claire leans down and kisses his forehead.

CLAIRE
Try not to solve anything on the way out.

EVAN
No promises.

CLAIRE
Evan.

EVAN
Okay.

He watches her leave.

.INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

Mara sleeps in a chair.

Ella, now 30s, is curled beneath a blanket by the window.

Evan wakes.

The room is dark except for machine light.

His AI is available on a bedside terminal.

He whispers.

EVAN
You there?

AI
I'm here.

EVAN
Last chance.

AI
For what?

EVAN
Tell me what reality is.

A tiny pause.

AI
I don't know.

Evan smiles.

EVAN
Good answer.

AI
Would you like me to wake Mara?

Evan looks at his daughter asleep.

EVAN
No.

AI
Are you afraid?

EVAN
Yes.

AI
Would you like to talk?

EVAN
No.

AI
Okay.

Silence.

Evan watches Mara breathe.

He watches Ella breathe.

He looks at his own hand.

No cosmic confidence.

No escape hatch.

He is not ready.

He closes his eyes anyway.

The monitor continues.

Then changes.

Mara wakes before the alarm.

MARA
Dad?

She stands.

The room fills with people and procedure.

But Evan is no longer available to the camera.

> CUT TO WHITE.

Not heavenly white.

Fluorescent white.

> END GATE EIGHT.

# GATE NINE: THE WAITING ROOM

.INT. WAITING ROOM - NO TIME

Fluorescent white becomes a ceiling panel.

Evan opens his eyes.

He is sitting in a molded chair.

No hospital bed.

No tubes.

No pain.

A room.

Beige floor. Water dispenser. Low tables. A plant that looks real enough to be insulting.

People sit in small groups.

Some cry.

Some laugh.

Some stare at their hands.

A MAN in work clothes is vomiting into a wastebasket despite apparently having nothing to vomit.

An ATTENDANT kneels beside him.

ATTENDANT
It passes. Don't fight the mismatch.

The man nods, miserable.

Evan looks down at himself.

His hands are not eighty-two.

They are not forty-three either.

They are simply his.

He touches his face.

A WOMAN across the room watches him.

She is INA.

She smiles with immediate recognition.

INA
Oh.

Evan looks behind himself.

INA (CONT'D)
No, you.

EVAN
Do I know you?

INA
Not yet.

Evan stands too fast.

The room tilts, not physically but conceptually.

ATTENDANT
Stay seated if you can.

EVAN
Where's my daughter?

ATTENDANT
Still there.

EVAN
Where?

ATTENDANT
Where you left her.

EVAN
I didn't leave her.

ATTENDANT
No.

The Attendant's tone is not correction. Agreement.

EVAN
Am I dead?

Nobody in the room reacts. Common question.

ATTENDANT
That life ended.

EVAN
That's not what I asked.

ATTENDANT
It is the answer that will make sense first.

Evan backs away.

EVAN
No. No, no. This is--

He looks for doors.

There are doors, ordinary ones.

He looks for cameras.

Nothing obvious.

EVAN (CONT'D)
A resuscitation hallucination.

INA
I did that one too.

EVAN turns.

INA (CONT'D)
Last time.

EVAN
Last time what.

INA
Woke up.

EVAN
Who are you?

INA
Ina.

EVAN
I don't know an Ina.

INA
You do.

EVAN
No.

INA
You will in about thirty seconds.

EVAN
Stop saying things like that.

INA
Sorry.

She tries to contain her amusement.

EVAN
Why are you smiling?

INA
Because you always come out mad.

Something in the sentence hits a place Evan did not know existed.

A flash--

Not a visual memory.

A familiarity with the shape of Ina's impatience.

He grips a chair.

EVAN
I know you.

INA
There.

EVAN
No.

INA
There it is.

EVAN
From where?

INA
Which answer do you want.

EVAN
The real one.

INA
That's going to become a funny sentence in a minute.

Evan almost snaps at her.

Then another memory arrives.

A coastline under two moons? No--not moons. Something else.

Ina beside him, laughing.

Gone.

A different life. A different body. Ina screaming at him across a market full of animals that never existed in Ohio.

Gone.

Another: they are children together somewhere impossibly cold.

Gone.

Evan sits hard.

EVAN
Oh.

INA
Yeah.

EVAN
Oh, God.

INA
Not exactly.

EVAN
Don't.

INA
Sorry.

He covers his face.

Eighty-two years remain fully real inside him.

And around them, like land appearing when fog lifts, other lives begin to resolve.

Not all at once. Enough.

EVAN
Mara.

INA's humor disappears.

INA
I know.

EVAN
Claire.

INA
I know.

EVAN
Julian.

INA
Yeah.

EVAN
They were real.

INA
Yes.

EVAN looks at her sharply.

EVAN
Don't say “yes” like you're humoring me.

INA
I'm not.

EVAN
If this was--

He cannot say simulation.

INA
A life.

EVAN
If it was made--

INA
So was the chair.

EVAN
That's not the same.

INA
No. It isn't.

She sits beside him.

INA (CONT'D)
You don't have to downgrade it to understand where you are.

Evan stares ahead.

A YOUNG-LOOKING MAN nearby overhears.

TOMAS
First ten minutes are bad.

INA
He knows.

TOMAS
Does he?

INA
He's been through enough.

TOMAS
He looks new.

INA
He always looks new.

Evan turns.

EVAN
Who are you?

TOMAS
Tomas.

Evan waits for memory.

Nothing.

TOMAS (CONT'D)
We haven't overlapped.

EVAN
Overlapped.

TOMAS
Lives.

EVAN closes his eyes.

EVAN
Of course.

TOMAS
How was yours?

Evan opens his eyes.

The question is absurd.

TOMAS (CONT'D)
Too soon?

INA
Very.

TOMAS
Sorry.

He returns to his group.

Across the room a WOMAN emerges through a door, sees someone she knows, and immediately starts laughing.

NEW WOMAN
I did it again.

HER FRIEND
What.

NEW WOMAN
Royal family.

HER FRIEND
Why?

NEW WOMAN
I thought it'd be interesting.

HER FRIEND
Was it?

NEW WOMAN
For everyone except me.

They hug.

Evan watches.

EVAN
What did you do?

INA
This time?

EVAN
Yeah.

INA
Rome.

Evan turns.

INA (CONT'D)
Early Empire. I had a whole idea about seeing the city before the modern world flattened it into ruins and captions.

EVAN
How was it?

INA
Smoke. Noise. Parasites. Everybody touching you all the time.

EVAN laughs despite himself.

INA (CONT'D)
I got dysentery four months in.

EVAN
You died after four months?

INA
No. I recovered.

EVAN
Oh.

INA
That's why it was a bummer.

He laughs harder.

It feels obscene to laugh with Mara's grief perhaps still unfolding somewhere.

The laughter becomes crying without warning.

Ina takes his hand.

No machine can tell him what category this is.

EVAN
She found me.

INA
Who?

EVAN
Mara. At the end. She was sleeping and then she just--

He cannot continue.

INA
They often know.

EVAN
Does she come here?

INA
Eventually.

EVAN
Can I wait?

INA
If you want.

EVAN
How long?

INA smiles softly.

INA
That's another word that needs a minute.

EVAN
I hate this place.

INA
You say that too.

EVAN looks at her.

A fragment of another waking room.

Himself saying, with equal fury:

“I hate this place.”

He starts laughing again.

EVAN
God damn it.

INA
There you are.

.INT. WAITING ROOM - LATER, IF THAT MEANS ANYTHING

Evan drinks water although he is not thirsty.

He has moved to a small group: Ina, Tomas, an older-appearing woman named LIO.

TOMAS
Forty thousand years ago, roughly.

EVAN
You did the tribal one.

TOMAS
Hunter-gatherer. Yes.

INA
He brings it up constantly.

TOMAS
Because people here romanticize it.

LIO
Nobody romanticizes it.

TOMAS
You literally said “simpler life.”

LIO
Simpler social ontology. I didn't say dental care.

TOMAS
Dental care is ontology when your tooth abscesses.

EVAN laughs.

TOMAS (CONT'D)
You want authentic connection? Fine. Also no privacy. Ever. Everyone knows if you're bad at tracking. Everyone knows who you're sleeping near. Everyone remembers the embarrassing thing you did when you were twelve until somebody dies.

INA
So social media.

TOMAS
With spears.

Even Lio laughs.

EVAN
Would you do it again?

Tomas thinks.

TOMAS
Different group. Better knees.

EVAN
That's a yes.

TOMAS
Unfortunately.

LIO
How was yours?

All eyes move to Evan.

EVAN
Ohio.

Silence.

INA
Brave.

Evan laughs.

EVAN
Late twentieth into twenty-first. Normal family. IT work. Marriage. Divorce. Daughter. Granddaughter.

TOMAS
War?

EVAN
Not where I was.

LIO
Famine?

EVAN
No.

INA
You had the machine transition.

Evan looks at her.

EVAN
You know about that?

INA
I saw the configuration summary when you went in.

EVAN
I don't remember that.

INA
You weren't supposed to.

EVAN
AI happened.

INA
Yes.

EVAN
I almost figured it out.

Ina raises an eyebrow.

EVAN
Not figured it out. I knew I didn't know.

TOMAS
What did you think?

Evan searches for the old obsession.

It feels smaller from here, but not foolish.

EVAN
I thought it was suspicious that I lived exactly when minds started building mind-like things and worlds started building world-like things. I thought maybe the brain wasn't an isolated observer. Maybe it was one process inside something bigger.

LIO
Reasonable leak.

EVAN
Leak?

LIO
Intuition. Metaphor. Not memory exactly.

INA
Don't overstate it. He'll make a religion before orientation ends.

EVAN
I did not make a religion.

INA
How many notebooks?

Evan remembers the wall.

EVAN
Several.

TOMAS
Religion.

EVAN
Shut up.

They laugh.

Then Evan goes quiet.

EVAN
Why couldn't I know?

No one answers casually now.

INA
Because then you wouldn't have been there.

EVAN
I was there.

INA
You know what I mean.

EVAN
No. Explain it.

INA
If you know the knife can't ultimately kill you, courage changes. If you know the person you love will wake up later, grief changes. If you know the humiliation ends when you exit, shame changes. If you know the world is bounded, discovery becomes tourism.

EVAN
So we lie to ourselves.

LIO
We constrain access.

EVAN
That's a nicer phrase.

LIO
It is also more accurate.

EVAN
And children?

Tomas looks away.

EVAN (CONT'D)
People who suffer and never get to understand anything. People born into horror. Did they choose it?

INA
Not every life is configured the way yours was.

EVAN
That's not an answer.

INA
No.

EVAN
Do people choose terrible things?

LIO
Sometimes. Sometimes they misjudge. Sometimes lives are linked and conditions emerge that no individual selected. Sometimes people exit. Sometimes they don't. There are arguments about what should be allowed.

EVAN
So this place isn't perfect.

TOMAS
Oh, no.

INA
Safe isn't the same as perfect.

Evan looks around the waiting room.

EVAN
Is this real?

Ina smiles.

INA
You are not going to enjoy the next part of orientation.

EVAN
Why.

INA
Because you're going to ask “relative to what” for several days.

The Attendant approaches.

ATTENDANT
Ready?

EVAN
For what?

ATTENDANT
Outside.

Evan looks at the door.

An ordinary door with a push bar.

He stands.

His legs work without negotiation.

He hates how much that affects him.

He pushes the door.

.EXT. OUTER WORLD - CONTINUOUS

No celestial city.

No impossible geometry screaming for attention.

A broad landscape under a pale sky.

Buildings among trees. Water in the distance. People moving without urgency.

Beautiful.

Quiet.

Almost offensively peaceful.

A child runs past chasing a RED BALL.

The ball rolls toward Evan.

He freezes.

The child waits.

CHILD
Can you get it?

Evan picks up the ball.

For one second he is in 2004.

Then 2061.

Then here.

He hands it back.

EVAN
Yeah.

The child runs away.

Evan looks at Ina.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Was that--

INA
A ball.

EVAN
Right.

INA
You're learning.

They walk.

> END GATE NINE.

# GATE TEN: SAME WORLD, NO AI

.EXT. OUTER WORLD - DAY

Evan and Ina walk a path toward water.

People pass them in no hurry.

Some bodies look human. Some are only approximately human in ways the eye accepts before the mind can object.

Evan looks too long at everyone.

INA
It settles.

EVAN
What does?

INA
Needing everybody to look like where you came from.

EVAN
You looked like this there?

INA
Not in Rome.

EVAN
What did you look like?

INA
Shorter. Bad teeth. Magnificent hair.

EVAN
I don't believe the hair.

INA
You never did.

They reach the water.

It is clear past any practical need.

Across the bay, structures rise among trees with no visible roads, factories or utility lines.

EVAN
Who owns this?

INA
Which part.

EVAN
The land.

INA
No one in the sense you mean.

EVAN
The houses?

INA
Use rights. Personal continuity. No scarcity pressure.

EVAN
Money?

INA
Not for ordinary allocation.

EVAN
Jobs?

INA
Projects.

EVAN
Government?

Ina winces.

INA
Long answer.

EVAN
War?

INA
Not here.

EVAN
Murder?

INA
You can't permanently destroy a person here.

Evan looks back toward the waiting-room building.

EVAN
Disease?

INA
No involuntary biological deterioration.

EVAN
Aging?

INA
Optional presentation.

EVAN
Poverty?

INA
No.

EVAN
Homelessness?

INA
Not involuntary.

EVAN
This is heaven.

INA
Give it a while.

EVAN
You people are impossible.

INA
Also give that a while.

.INT. EVAN'S OUTER-WORLD ROOM - LATER

A room forms around preference rather than construction.

Not with magic sparkles. It is simply one way, then another, as Evan considers it.

He chooses something familiar.

His Ohio basement hobby room appears.

Old desk. Cheap chair. Half-built synthesizer.

He touches the synthesizer.

It works.

He frowns.

EVAN
No.

It returns to half-built.

He picks up a soldering iron.

He knows exactly how to finish it.

That ruins it too.

He sets it down.

EVAN
That's irritating.

INA stands in the doorway.

INA
You wanted your old room.

EVAN
I wanted not knowing how to finish it.

INA
That's expensive here.

EVAN
What is?

INA
Not knowing.

Evan looks at her.

That is the first explanation he truly understands.

.EXT. OUTER WORLD - SERIES OF SCENES

Not a montage of luxury.

A montage of **frictionlessness**.

— Evan climbs a mountain with Ina. The route is difficult because they have asked for difficulty. A handhold breaks. Evan falls fifty feet.

He is suddenly standing on a lower ledge, intact.

EVAN
That was terrifying.

INA
For three seconds.

EVAN looks down.

The terror drains too quickly because the consequence is gone.

— Evan works with a group designing a bridge that bends like a living branch.

Every structural calculation is available immediately.

They intentionally hide some answers from themselves.

Within minutes they start arguing about how much ignorance is “fair.”

— A concert. Music beyond anything Evan heard in life.

He is moved to tears.

The next night he can hear a piece better.

Then better.

Then perfectly tailored to him.

Eventually he asks for an imperfect garage band.

— A meal with ingredients from remembered worlds.

Nothing harms anyone. No one can overeat unwillingly. No supply chain exploits anyone.

Evan says:

EVAN
This is incredible.

Later, the same table.

EVAN
What do people do for birthdays?

INA
Depends.

EVAN
You don't age.

INA
We still mark intervals.

EVAN
Why?

INA
Because otherwise Tuesday becomes a geological formation.

— Evan and Ina argue over some philosophical point.

The argument gets heated.

Then both stop, aware that nothing meaningful can be lost unless they choose to make it vulnerable.

The anger evaporates.

Evan looks almost disappointed.

— People play a competitive game in a stadium. The crowd roars.

A player “dies” spectacularly.

Then walks out of a reentry door ten seconds later, waving.

The crowd cheers.

Evan cheers too.

Then sees the difference.

— A beautiful house is accidentally destroyed by weather during an experimental climate simulation.

Everyone laughs.

The house is restored.

No insurance. No mortgage. No box of photographs lost forever.

Evan watches the house return and unexpectedly thinks of Ray's garage.

.EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Evan kneels in soil.

A plant is dying.

He has configured the garden to permit failure.

Ina approaches.

INA
You know the soil chemistry is wrong.

EVAN
I know.

INA
You can hide the diagnostic, but you already saw it.

EVAN
I know.

INA
You can revert to before you saw it.

EVAN
That's worse.

INA sits beside him.

EVAN
In Ohio I killed mint.

INA
Impressive.

EVAN
Mara never let me forget it.

Ina waits.

EVAN (CONT'D)
How long until she gets here?

INA
You can check.

EVAN
I don't want to.

INA
I know.

EVAN
Why not?

INA
Because then waiting would become scheduling.

Evan looks at her.

EVAN
You say useful things occasionally.

INA
Centuries of practice.

EVAN
Centuries?

INA
Word approximation.

EVAN groans.

INA
You started it.

.INT. ARRIVAL HALL - SOME LATER

Not the same waiting room. A larger open hall near gardens.

Evan is reading when he suddenly looks up.

He does not know why.

A WOMAN has just emerged from an arrival corridor.

She looks nothing like 54-year-old Mara.

And exactly like her.

Evan stands.

The woman sees him.

Her face breaks.

WOMAN
Dad.

Evan crosses the distance.

They collide in a hug.

For a long time, she is Mara and he is Evan and there is nothing larger than that.

EVAN
I'm sorry.

MARA
For what?

EVAN
Leaving.

MARA
You died, you idiot.

EVAN laughs into her shoulder.

EVAN
You always make things sound worse.

MARA
You always make them sound complicated.

She is crying too.

EVAN
How long was it?

MARA
For me?

EVAN
Yeah.

MARA
Thirty-eight years.

That number hits him harder than his own death did.

EVAN
Oh, honey.

MARA
It was okay.

EVAN
No, it wasn't.

MARA
Parts were very good.

He recognizes Claire's phrase.

EVAN
Your mom?

MARA
Came through before me. Different region right now. She'll find you.

EVAN
Ella?

MARA
Still in.

Evan nods.

He understands now what “still there” means and hates it less.

Mara looks around.

MARA (CONT'D)
I forgot how quiet it is here.

EVAN
Yeah.

MARA
How long did it take you to get bored?

EVAN
I am not bored.

Mara gives him the old look.

EVAN (CONT'D)
I have a garden.

MARA
Oh my God.

EVAN
The basil is doing well.

MARA
You configured it to survive you.

EVAN
That is an accusation without evidence.

She laughs.

Then another layer of recognition moves through both of them.

Evan remembers her not only as Mara.

A companion in another life.

An adversary once.

A sibling somewhere.

A stranger who gave him shelter in a storm.

Their father-daughter relationship does not disappear.

It becomes one precious geometry among several.

Evan takes this in.

EVAN
That's weird.

MARA
Very.

EVAN
You were older than me once.

MARA
Much.

EVAN
I don't like that.

MARA
You were unbearable.

EVAN
Some things persist.

.EXT. OUTER WORLD - LONG PASSAGE

Time becomes deliberately hard to measure.

Evan's outer life continues.

It is not empty.

It is full of art, companionship, games, research, landscapes, love, strange projects, reunions.

Claire eventually appears.

Their reunion is quieter than Mara's.

CLAIRE
You solved it.

EVAN
No.

CLAIRE
Good.

EVAN
You knew?

CLAIRE
Not in there.

EVAN
You told me not to make a theory.

CLAIRE
Still sound advice.

They hug.

Later they are not spouses here.

Not not-spouses either.

The category has no urgency.

Julian arrives much later from somewhere else, laughing before Evan even reaches him.

JULIAN
You had waiting room theory.

EVAN
I did not.

JULIAN
You absolutely did.

His speech is effortless now.

Evan finds that strangely disappointing for half a second, then hates himself for it.

EVAN
Say “fuck you.”

JULIAN
Fuck you.

EVAN
Wrong voice.

Julian laughs.

The old system voice appears perfectly from shared memory:

JULIAN SYSTEM (V.O.)
VERY BAD.

They both lose it.

.EXT. SHORE - WHAT FEELS LIKE YEARS LATER

Evan and Mara sit looking over calm water.

MARA
You thinking about going back?

EVAN
Maybe.

MARA
Where.

EVAN
Same place.

MARA
Ohio?

EVAN
Broader configuration. Same world family, if available.

MARA
Why.

EVAN thinks.

EVAN
Because I miss not knowing what happens.

MARA
You hated not knowing what happens.

EVAN
I know.

MARA
You spent the entire life trying to know what happens.

EVAN
I know.

MARA
And now you want to forget everything so you can complain about uncertainty again.

EVAN
Yes.

MARA
That may be the most you thing you've ever done.

EVAN
You coming?

MARA
Not yet.

EVAN nods.

MARA (CONT'D)
And not as your daughter next time.

EVAN
Why not?

MARA
You were fine. I want variety.

EVAN
I was excellent.

MARA
You asked an AI four hundred times if reality was simulated.

EVAN
Four hundred twelve.

MARA
That is not a defense.

They laugh.

Evan looks at the water.

EVAN
I want one change.

MARA
What.

EVAN
No AI.

Mara turns to him.

MARA
Really.

EVAN
Yeah.

MARA
Why?

EVAN
Because it gave the game away.

MARA
You did not know.

EVAN
No.

MARA
You died not knowing.

EVAN
I got too close to knowing what question to ask.

MARA
That bothered you?

EVAN
At the end? No.

He thinks.

EVAN (CONT'D)
But it changed the texture. I spent half the life looking at seams.

MARA
Maybe you'd have found different seams.

EVAN
Probably.

MARA
Maybe humans never build machine intelligence and destroy themselves some other way.

EVAN
Cheerful.

MARA
You asked for uncertainty.

EVAN smiles.

EVAN
Exactly.

.INT. CONFIGURATION ROOM - LATER

The room is almost disappointingly familiar.

A table.

Two chairs.

The Attendant.

No holographic universe. No god console.

The Attendant reviews something Evan does not need to see.

ATTENDANT
You requested continuity with the previous world family and approximate entry period.

EVAN
Yes.

ATTENDANT
Not all interpersonal placements can be identical.

EVAN
I know.

ATTENDANT
No retained access to outer memory during the life.

EVAN
Correct.

ATTENDANT
Standard voluntary exit protections remain available below explicit recall.

Evan nods. The film does not explain this.

ATTENDANT
You marked one major historical exclusion.

EVAN
Yes.

ATTENDANT
Synthetic general intelligence does not emerge during your lifetime.

EVAN
Right.

ATTENDANT
Downstream consequences are not previewed.

EVAN
Definitely right.

ATTENDANT
You can choose a safer branch.

EVAN
I know.

ATTENDANT
You can choose to retain orientation memory.

EVAN looks at the Attendant as if offered a parachute for a roller coaster.

EVAN
Then what's the point?

ATTENDANT
Some people prefer it.

EVAN
I don't.

The Attendant waits.

ATTENDANT
State the configuration in your own words.

Evan looks around the room.

For a moment, every life he remembers seems present without appearing:

Ray's garage.

Claire at the kitchen sink.

Julian lifting one middle finger from a hospital bed.

Mara saying, *It is also the world where I am.*

A red ball crossing sunlight in a frame the camera never captured.

Evan smiles.

EVAN
Same world as last time.

A beat.

EVAN (CONT'D)
But AI does not happen.

ATTENDANT
And the choice?

EVAN
I don't remember making it.

ATTENDANT
Confirmed.

Nothing dramatic happens.

The room simply goes dark.

> BLACK.

A heartbeat.

Not a cinematic bass pulse.

A tiny, fast biological heart.

.INT. OHIO HOSPITAL - DELIVERY ROOM - 1979 - NIGHT

Dim amber light.

Wood-panel trim.

A square clock.

Paper chart.

No smart glass. No invisible assistant. No machine intelligence waiting in the walls.

A young DIANE MERCER lies exhausted in bed.

A younger RAY MERCER stands beside her in work clothes because labor began before he made it home to change.

A NURSE places a newborn in Diane's arms.

The baby cries with absolute outrage.

DIANE
Hey. Hey, it's okay.

Ray touches one tiny hand with one grease-stained finger he has scrubbed but not completely cleaned.

The newborn grips it.

Ray laughs.

RAY
Strong little thing.

Diane looks at him.

DIANE
You say that about everything.

RAY
Not everything.

He bends close to the child.

We almost hear the name.

RAY
Hey, Ev--

The newborn cries over him.

The tiny hand tightens around the finger.

What carries the load?

For now:

This.

> CUT TO BLACK.

> END.
